window.harnessChunks["rows-cf35aeb4fcbe997a.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-29T18:38:58Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/china-qijizhifeng/agentic-harness-engineering\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agentic Harness Engineering</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-29\">2026-09-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/china-qijizhifeng/agentic-harness-engineering/tree/8b2a55d97590363fe50c3cc6b5e833b020a4bb4c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8b2a55d97590363fe50c3cc6b5e833b020a4bb4c\">8b2a55d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: solve environment-facing software tasks by inspecting a target repository, choosing shell-based edits/tests/actions, and iterating from tool results until the task is completed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous software tasks, repository state, compiler/test failures, command output, file contents, runtime errors and task-specific implementation constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the substantive next repository inspection, edit, build or test action from the live task/tool context. - Decision owner: the configured coding-agent LLM in agents/codeagentsimple, invoked through the shipped NexAU agent loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runshellcommand; NexAU execution loop; E2B/Harbor task environment; in-memory tracing; model/provider configuration; iteration limits. - Closure path: benchmark task/repository state enters coding agent → model selects shell action → command changes or inspects the task environment → output/test result returns to the model → subsequent action adapts until completion/termination → external verifier evaluates the resulting task state. - Boundary reachability: this coding-agent loop is the default operational harness evaluated and evolved by AHE, not a repository-development-only helper. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor leaves shell and benchmark machinery but removes the open-ended selection of implementation/debugging actions. - Evidence: codeagent.yaml; systemprompt.md; evolve.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Harbor/E2B and model inference are external dependencies; AHE deliberately wires the model actor into the first-party coding harness and closes tool results back into that actor.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 loop was established that attenuates a concrete interference/conflict/oscillation between distinct operational S1 units at the selected recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: AHE can run many benchmark rollouts and optional evolution variants concurrently, but the reviewed distribution does not establish a shared operational disturbance among them that is sensed and attenuated as S2. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S2 at this recursion. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Harbor concurrency; thread pools; progress/tracer locks; git worktrees for Best-of-N variants; deterministic winner selection; task/variant result aggregation. - Closure path: no S2-specific closure established. Parallel benchmark trials are independent evaluations, while Best-of-N worktrees deliberately isolate candidate adaptations and later select one; neither path demonstrates coordination feedback that changes distinct S1 units to attenuate an inter-S1 disturbance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: concurrency and isolation are runtime/experiment support; no autonomous or constructor-owned S2 decision is needed because the required S2 disturbance/coordination relation is not established. - Evidence: evolve.py; configs/base.yaml. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generated/evolved harnesses may themselves contain multi-agent coordination; those later candidate designs are not automatically part of AHE&#39;s own frozen organizational closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party whole-system current-control loop was established that observes live operational commitments/resources/priorities and makes bounded present-time interventions across the selected organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the orchestrator observes evaluation status, scores, timeouts and historical regressions, but its consequential changes are primarily future harness adaptations rather than regulation of live coding-task commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S3. Target pass rate, maximum iterations, concurrency and timeouts are configured thresholds/enforcement rather than a discretionary whole-current decision right. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: iteration loop; current pass-rate/statistics; task stability; experiment/job timeouts; configured concurrency; best-ever bookkeeping; notifications; Best-of-N result selection. - Closure path: no S3-specific closure established. Evaluation evidence flows into Debugger/Evolve analysis and then into changes to the next harness capability; that path is credited under S3/S4. Timeout/termination and target-threshold checks enforce predefined rules rather than making an open current-control allocation/commitment judgment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Evolve Agent owns adaptation choices, not a distinct current-control function over live operational commitments. Deterministic orchestration supplies execution support but does not close S3 under the Profile&#39;s whole-current test. - Evidence: evolve.py; configs/base.yaml; evolveprompt.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: if a future AHE mode gives an agent authority to reallocate or preempt live task resources/commitments from a whole-system current view, that would warrant reassessment; no such frozen first-party closure was found here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently reconcile coding-agent operational trajectories with external verifier evidence, diagnose root cause beyond ordinary pass/fail reporting, and return those findings into the evolution decision path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: superficial task outcomes or the coding agent&#39;s own trajectory may not reveal why a task failed, timed out or regressed; an independent evidence path is needed to distinguish agent behavior from verifier-grounded failure reality. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge the likely root cause and salient failure mechanism from raw traces plus verifier evidence, producing an audit finding used by the subsequent harness-change decision. - Decision owner: the Agent Debugger QA LLM invoked through bundled adb ask; it is a separate analysis actor from the audited coding-agent LLM path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bundled Agent Debugger source; per-task trace collection; external verifier reward/test-output collection; separate debugger LLM configuration; analysis overview/detail artifacts; query injection into Evolve Agent. - Closure path: coding agent executes task → Harbor/verifier produces reward/test output and trace is persisted → Agent Debugger independently reads raw trace plus verifier evidence → debugger produces root-cause analysis → adboverview is injected into the Evolve Agent query → Evolve Agent uses the finding to choose workspace changes → subsequent coding-agent operations run the changed harness. - Boundary reachability: agentdebugger.enabled: true in the shipped base configuration, bundled source can be installed automatically when adb is absent, and runsingleexperiment directly invokes the analysis before evolution. - Claim being audited: the operational account implicit in the coding-agent trajectory and ordinary benchmark outcome — whether/why the performed behavior actually satisfies the task, times out, or causes a verifier-grounded failure/regression. - Ordinary reporting path: Harbor statistics expose pass/fail/exception/reward and AHE records the coding-agent trace; these results alone feed iteration statistics and history. - Complementary access path: Agent Debugger receives the raw/cleaned coding-agent trace together with verifier/test-stdout.txt and reward evidence; AHE explicitly notes that this external verifier output is produced after the agent finishes and is never seen by the coding agent. - Independence boundary: the Debugger runs as a separate QA-agent process/configuration over persisted evidence and writes analysis artifacts, not the audited coding workspace. Its evidence access includes external verifier output unavailable to the S1 actor; the Evolve Agent remains the separate actor that can modify the harness after receiving the finding. - Who acts on findings: the Evolve Agent; AHE inserts Debugger analysis into section 4 of the evolution query, after which the Evolve Agent selects and applies evidence-backed harness edits used by later trials. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the Debugger LLM leaves raw traces and verifier outputs but removes the open-ended root-cause judgment. The deterministic code only assembles evidence and routes the finding; the audit judgment is model-owned. - Evidence: evolve.py; configs/base.yaml; agent-debugger-cli/source. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external verifier logic itself is not credited as AHE S3 ownership; the credited audit is AHE&#39;s distinct Debugger judgment over trace plus verifier-ground-truth evidence and its wired return to the Evolve Agent.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: sense external performance and current/future-relevant harness techniques, generate adaptation options, and autonomously install persistent harness changes that alter capability in later coding operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: benchmark failures/regressions, unstable task behavior, ineffective prior changes, evolving framework internals, and newly observed coding-agent architectures/tools/ablations can make the current harness inadequate for future tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which prompt/tool/middleware/skill/sub-agent/memory changes to make, retain, revise or roll back based on evaluation, debugger, historical and research evidence. - Decision owner: the Evolve Agent LLM for substantive adaptation choices; Explore-Agent LLMs autonomously produce external/source knowledge used as adaptation evidence. Optional Best-of-N selection deterministically chooses among already agent-generated variants by measured performance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: evaluation statistics and task flips; changeevaluation.json; best-ever/history data; Agent Debugger findings; Explore Agent web/source research; registered research skills; writable evolution workspace; git commits/tags/worktrees; subsequent Harbor evaluation. - Closure path: current harness runs external benchmark tasks → AHE records performance/traces and Debugger findings while Explore Agent gathers source/web evidence → Evolve Agent synthesizes this evidence into concrete harness edits → first-party tools write those edits into the persistent workspace → next iteration executes the changed coding harness → new outcomes validate, revise or trigger rollback/pivot decisions. - Boundary reachability: Explore Agent and Agent Debugger are enabled in the default base configuration; the Evolve Agent and persistent workspace mutation are the core documented evaluate → analyze → improve loop. - External distinction: external benchmark/verifier outcomes expose task failures and regressions, while the enabled Explore Agent reads remote framework source plus current external coding-agent architecture/benchmark/web sources. - Future / prospective distinction: Explore Agent is instructed to search recent state-of-the-art designs and ablations for implementable techniques; cross-iteration change evaluation, stability and regression evidence also distinguish which adaptations are likely to improve rather than damage later task capability. - Adaptation option generated: the Evolve Agent may create or modify system prompts, tool descriptions/implementations, middleware, skills, sub-agents and long-term memory, and may choose rollback/pivot when prior changes are harmful or ineffective. - Path back into current capability / S3: chosen edits are written into AHE&#39;s persistent workspace, committed/tagged, and that workspace becomes the coding-agent harness evaluated on the next iteration. Thus the prospective decision changes the capability currently available to later S1 operations even though no separate S3 loop is credited. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the orchestrator supplies evidence and writable boundaries, but the Evolve Agent makes the open-ended semantic choice of what capability change to implement. Removing that model actor leaves metrics and files but not materially the same adaptation decision. - Evidence: README.md; evolve.py; evolveprompt.md; configs/base.yaml; exploreagent/run.py; webagent/prompt.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external benchmark, web and source systems provide evidence rather than owning AHE&#39;s adaptation decision. Best-of-N is optional and not required for the S4 result; the ordinary default evolution loop already closes adaptation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision loop with legitimate ultimate authority and return-to-operation closure was established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: AHE has an explicit pass@1 optimization objective, protected prompt rules, workspace write boundaries and model/config restrictions, but these are authored constraints on evolution rather than a runtime identity-policy adjudication loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S5-specific right was found to resolve an identity/ultimate-policy issue or to change the organization&#39;s ultimate purpose/constitution at runtime. - Decision owner: not established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configured targetpassrate; Evolve-Agent prompt constraints; write-only-within-workspace rule; prohibition on changing LLM config; protected original system-prompt rules; static strategy constraints; experiment configuration. - Closure path: no S5-specific escalation-and-return path established. The Evolve Agent optimizes within the existing pass@1 objective and authored boundaries; it does not escalate an unresolved identity/ultimate-policy question to a legitimate ultimate authority whose ruling returns into later operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: autonomous optimization inside a fixed objective is S4-capability adaptation, not ownership of ultimate policy. Static operator-authored constraints remain configuration even when strongly enforced. - Evidence: evolveprompt.md; configs/base.yaml; evolve.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a human running AHE can edit configuration or source out of band, but generic maintainer authority is not a first-party operational S5 closure path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f441a0b21a7198cd49ebb24082c1bf643b5bc21a/assessments/ahe.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f441a0b21a7198cd49ebb24082c1bf643b5bc21a\">f441a0b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T18:08:16+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/HaseebKhalid1507/SynapsCLI\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SynapsCLI</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/HaseebKhalid1507/SynapsCLI/tree/62853b06777fcc16db4b142d535a99e23da4b207\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 62853b06777fcc16db4b142d535a99e23da4b207\">62853b0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: foreground and optionally spawned worker agents absorb open-ended coding/terminal task variety through repeated model decisions, tool execution and observed results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertain file/process/tool results, task decomposition and changing intermediate evidence in each worker&#39;s environment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose model-requested tools and follow-on tasks from returned observations, including whether to continue or finish. - Decision owner: model-driven foreground/worker agent inside the shipped runtime; an external model endpoint supplies inference but the tool-action loop is first-party wired. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapters, tool registry, stream events, sessions, budgets, daemon, context checkpointing and cancellation. - Closure path: goal/history → LLM tool choice → first-party tool execution → tool result added to history → next provider round / final response. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: tool choice and next action are not prewritten as a deterministic workflow; the model acts on feedback, including inside subagent runtime. - Evidence: crates/agent-engine/src/runtime/stream.rs, tools/subagent/start.rs and README (pinned links above). - Basis: direct source-first control-flow inspection. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: task correctness depends on the selected model, tools and user permissions; a model-less session does not exercise S1. - Boundary reachability: shipped chat/TUI/RPC foreground stream and spawned subagent stream, using the standard bundled tool registry.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate concrete file-write interference between concurrently active autonomous work units within a shared workgroup. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent foreground/worker modifications to the same file/path, plus worker output arriving after foreground completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose worker isolation/write scopes and adjust work after a collision/reconciliation signal; worker-collect/reconcile is a separate foreground coordination act. - Decision owner: foreground LLM when it selects writepolicy for subagentstart, steers/rescopes work and decides what to reconcile; host registry enforces matching scopes and returns a refusal. This does not credit deterministic scope matching itself as autonomous judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WorkerWritePolicy, checkforegroundwrite, overlap predicate, write/edit rejection and subagent completion gate. - Closure path: foreground chooses nonoverlapping scopes → concurrent agent work → overlapping foreground write is rejected with a concrete error → model receives that result and must avoid/reconcile the collision before continuing; terminal worker result is collected and reconciled before completion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: unlike mere subagent delegation, the scope choice and handling of a concrete collision remain in the model&#39;s next-action loop; host rejection is evidence of an actual structurally implemented inter-S1 interference path. - Evidence: tools/subagent/start.rs, agent-core/src/orchestration.rs, tools/write.rs, tools/edit.rs, tools/subagent/collect.rs (pinned links above). - Basis: source-structural coordination witness, without an observed live multi-agent collision. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: isolated worktree/read-only modes avoid the collision structurally, and incorrect agent scope declarations may fail to protect files outside their scope. - Distinct S1 units: an autonomous foreground model loop and an independently streamed subagent model loop share a task workspace; further workers may run concurrently. - Inter-S1 disturbance: foreground write/edit can target paths that a still-running worker declares writable, risking simultaneous edits to one work product. - Attenuating coordination relation: model-chosen writepolicy scope/isolation and registry&#39;s active-worker overlap check block a colliding foreground write. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the foreground receives the failed write as a tool result and can steer, collect or reconcile worker work before retrying or rescoping. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the exact overlap predicate and file-write rejection regulate concurrent operational interference, beyond mere messaging or dispatch. - Boundary reachability: bundled subagent dispatch and file-write tools use the same orchestration policy at runtime, without a plugin or downstream workflow definition.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate current commitments and accountability across active subagent work on behalf of the whole foreground task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: slow/stalled or diverging workers, exhausted concurrency/total-worker capacity, finished-but-unreconciled work and conflicting current implementation responsibilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect worker statuses and partial output, steer or resume a worker, decide which work to dispatch and whether results are reconciled with the foreground objective. - Decision owner: foreground model exercising bundled subagentstatus/steer/collect/resume tools; user may intervene, while the registry only tracks/enforces quotas and completion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model catalog/authorization, typed worker roles, worker status telemetry, runtime handle registry, budget/worker-count gates and prompt supervision guidance. - Closure path: model dispatches several current commitments → reads status/progress/completion → chooses steering/resumption or integration → collected result updates current task context and explicit reconciliation releases completion gate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: host logic does not choose which task is off-track or how to revise it; the foreground actor observes all subagents and has real steer/dispatch/collect authority over their current work. - Evidence: tools/subagent/start.rs, tools/subagent/collect.rs, agent-engine/src/orchestration.rs, agent-core/src/orchestration.rs, bundled supervision prompt and stream completion gate (pinned links above). - Basis: reachable first-party orchestration tools and feedback path. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: foreground supervision is prompt-guided and optional in single-agent mode; watcher restart/budget enforcement alone is not S3 agent ownership. - Whole-system current view: foreground can inspect all of its active worker handles, states, partial outputs and collected results through the shipped orchestration tools. - Current-control decision scope: model can dispatch, set role/write policy, steer, resume and reconcile current work against the foreground outcome; host applies hard budget/limit gates. - Boundary reachability: standard foreground tool registry wires subagent supervision and orchestration, and feedback returns into the same model stream.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no standard-distribution independent complementary access that challenges an S1 claim and returns audit findings into control is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: runtime traces, status and optional reviewer subagents can reveal errors, but are ordinary task/supervision paths. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently select an operational claim, inspect distinct evidence and return a corrective finding to current control. - Decision owner: not supplied by the standard distribution; a foreground agent/user may request an optional reviewer, without a guaranteed independent challenge channel. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagent reviewer role, partial-output/status reads, telemetry, tool events and debug logs. - Closure path: ordinary worker report → foreground inspection or optional prompted review; no dedicated independent audit access and controlled corrective return is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: typed reviewer capability and logs do not establish S3 independence or a different view of operational reality. - Evidence: tools/subagent/start.rs, tools/subagent/collect.rs, agent-engine/src/orchestration.rs, README. - Basis: bounded structural negative review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a specifically designed downstream reviewer workgroup may establish S3 and requires separate source evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no packaged external/future sensing → adaptation option → operational capability-change loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: models/providers, task feedback, package/plugin capabilities and crash recovery are present-task or maintenance concerns. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge future environmental differences and revise the agent system&#39;s future capabilities in conversation with S3. - Decision owner: user/operator/plugin author or downstream agent application; not a first-party S4 role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider catalog, plugins/extensions, MCP, watcher config reload, memory/compaction and project prompts. - Closure path: tools/model/config can change on external instruction, but the repository does not ship a prospective model of environment and a dedicated adaptation option-to-present-control return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: context persistence, plugin extensibility, task planning and deterministic config reload are not prospective organizational intelligence by themselves. - Evidence: README, src/watcher/supervisor.rs, crates/agent-engine/src/runtime/stream.rs. - Basis: bounded structural negative review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: some specific user-created long-running watchers might implement future adaptation; not inferred from runtime generality.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity- or ultimate-policy decision path with legitimate authority and returned governing closure is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model eligibility, session budgets, credential scope, system prompts, tool confirmations and worker write rules constrain operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the identity, legitimate authority boundaries and ultimate policy if operational/future tensions require resolution. - Decision owner: user/operator/configurator at a wider boundary; runtime enforces predefined limits and explicit session grants. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DelegationPolicy, CrossProviderGrant, EnforcementMode, credential broker, user approval, prompt and role budgets. - Closure path: user-supplied settings/grants → deterministic enforcement → agent operation; no identity-level issue is routed to ultimate authority and returned as a revised policy decision in the shipped workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: policy text and runtime authorization cannot be promoted to S5 merely because they constrain agent autonomy. - Evidence: agent-core/src/orchestration.rs, README, src/watchersession.rs. - Basis: bounded structural negative review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: parent organizations may impose real S5 through an embedding deployment, outside the frozen standard-distribution boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b0dbd91d81fbf7b61e6356ed7ebb418c63a9eebf/assessments/sy/synapscli.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b0dbd91d81fbf7b61e6356ed7ebb418c63a9eebf\">b0dbd91 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T19:32:58+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/epicsagas/epic-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Epic Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/epicsagas/epic-harness/tree/1f3823af536b1f56b3cb8af90cc8ea77ef71c4bd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1f3823af536b1f56b3cb8af90cc8ea77ef71c4bd\">1f3823a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: model-directed software task execution from request/spec through task decomposition, code/tool intervention, test observation and local repair. - Disturbance / variety regulated: varied feature requests, test failures, codebase changes, missing context and integration feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select each next code/tool step, diagnose a failed test or review and revise artifacts until acceptance criteria or escalation conditions are reached. - Decision owner: host model running first-party /orbit and /go policies, with spawned specialist model agents owning scoped implementation decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: installed plugin skills, Claude/Codex/agy agent-host tools, TDD protocol, Git worktree, test runner, persistent orbit state and Rust hook state. - Closure path: user task → generated spec/role work → model-chosen edits/tests → test/reviewer evidence → model retry/adjust/stop → PR or bounded request for user decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model agents make changing next-step decisions; Rust&#39;s storage and hook checks do not replace model-driven work. - Evidence: /orbit, /go, README, src/orchestrate/mod.rs (links above). - Basis: first-party installed protocol and executable host reachability. - Confidence: medium-high for supported mode. - Caveats: runtime depends on installed host permissions/tools and the provider; no live E2E/quality certification was performed. - Boundary reachability: plugin manifests and installation load the first-party skills, and /orbit directly invokes model-host workers and returns their observations to its stateful pipeline.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent incompatible concurrently editing builder S1 agents from modifying the same file scope without isolation or sequencing. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel tasks targeting identical files, integration hazards and duplicate build work across worker contexts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: serialize overlapping tasks or isolate such workers in separate Git worktrees; permit independent files to proceed in parallel. - Decision owner: constructor-prescribed file-intersection and worktree isolation rules in /go and /orbit, executed by the model orchestration rather than an unrestricted autonomous policy setter. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-task modifies list, dependency graph, worktree isolation, agent result states, integration test and multi-agent review. - Closure path: conflicting pending S1 tasks → overlap/ordering decision → deferred or isolated worker executes → integration results feed later tasks/review, avoiding clobbering simultaneous edits. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the S2 relation targets identified shared-resource interference, beyond generic agent messaging or delegation; the governing interference rule is constructor-owned. - Distinct S1 units: independently executing TDD builder subagents in one /go multi-task plan. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two parallel model workers edit a common file, causing merge/working-tree collision and invalid integration. - Attenuating coordination relation: explicit Tasks modifying same files → serialize or use worktree isolation, with per-task overlap checked before dispatch. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: serialized workers only run after predecessors settle; isolated workers return changed trees/results for integration and failed integration feeds the repair loop. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the source names an actual concurrent-write conflict and prescribes isolation/serialization conditional on overlap, altering S1 execution to attenuate interference. - Evidence: /go conflict analysis and parallel execution table, /orbit worktree protocol. - Basis: concrete first-party orchestration constraints and returned integration path. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: the rule is a skill-level scheduling prescription; no universal cross-process conflict detector or measured failure rate is established. - Boundary reachability: /orbit calls /go, which requires overlap-aware planning before spawning the installed host model workers.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: model-led whole-pipeline current control over specs, tasks, worker results, verification, retries and PR readiness. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing task scope, failing audits/tests, missing context, partial worker outcomes, CI regressions and crash recovery state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose direct/council mode, integrate task status, dispatch and retry workers, decide phase transition or escalate a repeated failure. - Decision owner: /orbit model orchestration persona, operating the whole current work run under first-party rules and limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persisted PIPELINE-.json, phase history, worktree lock, /go integration, /audit report, optional /eval, CI checks, Rust orchestration events. - Closure path: goal plus whole-run state → model selects plan and workers → reads every stage&#39;s evidence → repairs failed phases or returns final PR/report/human pause. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current portfolio decisions are model policy within /orbit; Rust state management and audits supply controls and evidence but do not author the task-level strategy. - Whole-system current view: scope, phase history, worktree state, spec/AC coverage, task result states, cumulative audit failures, optional regression results and PR/CI state. - Current-control decision scope: which model subagents and phases run next, whether to retry/replan, and when the current workflow is ready to advance to ship or return to the user. - Evidence: /orbit full phase and recovery protocol, /go integration, src/orchestrate/mod.rs. - Basis: directly specified current orchestrator decision and closure rights. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: not autonomous authority over final GitHub merge or business-wide operations; EPICORCHESTRATION advanced directives are opt-in. - Boundary reachability: /orbit is a shipped runnable entry and sequentially reads/writes pipeline state, explicitly consuming audit and worker outcomes.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: strict-mode separate code, security/performance and test/AC investigators challenge the current build&#39;s claimed completion, returning independent evidence into /orbit control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: missed defects, builder self-certification, false-positive pass claims, review anchoring and hidden spec/test gaps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently inspect artifacts and determine PASS/WARN/FAIL findings that block or redirect current orchestration. - Decision owner: distinct fresh-context audit agents under the first-party audit --strict protocol, not the building agent; orchestrator then owns implementation of corrective action. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: artifact-only diff/spec input, builder session-ID exclusion, blind independent mode findings, tests, lint/security checks, dedup and synthesized audit report. - Closure path: S1 output/S3 completion claim → separate blind audit modes independently inspect actual diff/tests/acceptance criteria → synthesized finding → /orbit gates ship or triggers up to three corrective S1 cycles. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: these are independent model reviewers using complementary code/security/test access and returning a decision into S3, not merely the same builder reading its own self-report. - Claim being audited: the build orchestration&#39;s statement that all spec requirements are covered, implementation quality is adequate and a PR is ready. - Ordinary reporting path: /go&#39;s builder-submitted DONE/test summary and /orbit&#39;s own phase status. - Complementary access path: independently scoped diff/spec inspection, full test execution and security-review paths deliberately blinded to builder narrative in --strict mode. - Independence boundary: fresh audit agent instances cannot be the builder, cannot see builder session history or other reviewers&#39; conclusions before synthesis; explicit opt-in trust-isolation protocol. - Who acts on findings: /orbit consumes synthesized PASS/WARN/FAIL; failing findings cause repair/re-audit or user escalation, rather than being silently archived. - Evidence: skills/audit/SKILL.md strict audit mode, /orbit Step 4/5 and skills/eval/SKILL.md optional regression checks. - Basis: mode-specific structural independence, complementary evidence route and mandatory corrective return. - Confidence: medium; static source evidence of supported strict mode, not a verified independent deployed audit run. - Caveats: default audit lacks the explicit strict isolation promises; S3 credit applies only to reachable --strict configuration, and external CI/human review independence is not presumed. - Boundary reachability: the shipped /audit skill exposes --strict, its three independent model reviewer modes return the report consumed by the installed /orbit verdict/retry gate; a user/engagement setting must enable strict mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: multi-session evolution adapts local skills from past failures; it does not establish prospective external-environment sensing and strategic future organizational capability decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: recurrent failed commands, regressions, ineffective prompts and yet-untried skill edit types. - Decisive decision or feedback right: investigate external future changes/opportunities, select strategic adaptations and return them into the current S3 operational capability. - Decision owner: Rust deterministic pattern/variant pipeline and optional host synthesis for local skill repair; no independent future-environment intelligence agent found. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: post-invocation reflect, historical failure patterns, adaptation landscape, skill variants, seesaw no-regression gate and rollback. - Closure path: past local observations → failure classification/proposed skill patch → test/rollback/reload next session; no distinct external future view and strategic option selection. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: evolving a response skill from local historical errors is useful operational learning but not sufficient for VSM outside-and-then S4, even if branded &#39;self-evolving&#39;. - Evidence: /evolve, src/evolve/analysis.rs, src/evolve/planner.rs, src/evolve/seesaw.rs, hooks.json. - Basis: external-vs-internal feedback discrimination. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: future releases could add environmental scouting; the pinned revision is not credited for hypothetical paths.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: scope/permission gates, installed skill identity and human STOP choices constrain code actions but do not establish first-party ultimate identity and policy adjudication. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsafe tool use, permission failures, repeated audit failures, runtime installation variance and disputed user intent. - Decisive decision or feedback right: legitimately decide overarching system purpose and highest-level policy conflicts, returning binding identity choices to operations. - Decision owner: external user, plugin maintainers and host/provider permissions supply rules; the harness follows them and pauses for user decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: preflight lock, user pause on three failed audits, host permission model, plugin config, worktree restrictions and PR review. - Closure path: externally given goal and rules → installed operating checks, model&#39;s bounded behavior or human pause; no first-party ultimate-policy adjudication loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ability to create a PR or edit locally evolved skills is not constitutional identity authority over user/system goals. - Evidence: /orbit user gates, README installation/governance, src/orchestrate/mod.rs user-directed pause/cancel. - Basis: separation of external mandates and software enforcement. - Confidence: high for bounded S5 exclusion. - Caveats: separate human governance may be S5 in a larger assembled organization; it is not automatically imported here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d3d893bec106736a19579244c6b6eb0843950201/assessments/ep/epic-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d3d893bec106736a19579244c6b6eb0843950201\">d3d893b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-25T16:13:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/getkimchi/kimchi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kimchi</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-25\">2026-10-25</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/getkimchi/kimchi/tree/65dfefb12a177e60942dc8fde87eeabe02d006af\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 65dfefb12a177e60942dc8fde87eeabe02d006af\">65dfefb &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce a code change, tests, analysis or research artifact for a bounded project task using model-directed tools. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfamiliar source, implementation alternatives, failing tests/builds, file content, context limits and differing task requests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The Builder chooses the actual implementation and tests, the Fixer chooses repairs from findings, and Explorer/Researcher choose task-relevant observations; their executed tools affect returned artifacts. - Decision owner: Model-backed first-party Kimchi worker personas invoked by AgentManager and independent createAgentSession calls. External model inference/SDK generic loop is dependency, not credited as a separate Kimchi governance actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model-role routing, shared workspace, tool allowlists, worker turns/tokens/time budgets, background concurrency limits, session files, provider runtime and result transport. - Closure path: Parent task/spec → first-party Agent tool → Kimchi manager creates a configured worker session → model chooses read/write/bash actions → source/tests/report or blocked result returns to parent. - Boundary reachability: Enabled first-party extensions.agents and orchestration prompt are included in the regular CLI; multi-model has a hardcoded true global default. The actor path does not require a custom application to instantiate the extension or a separate developer-implemented worker. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the worker&#39;s model-mediated decisions leaves tool execution, limits and session transport, but no owner that chooses the code to write or the corrections to implement. Do not transfer upstream pi-mono&#39;s generic agent internals to Kimchi&#39;s organizational classification. - Evidence: Builder and Fixer personas; manager spawn; worker session construction. - Basis: Positive pinned first-party path. - Confidence: High for shipped autonomy wiring, not generated artifact quality. - Caveats: Single-model can run pi-mono directly with no autonomous role decomposition; the standard distribution&#39;s default multi-model mode supplies the cited positive S1 path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Would attenuate an evidenced interference or oscillation between distinct operational Builder/Fixer/other S1 units without merely commanding task phases. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Possible concurrent edits to shared files, incompatible worker assumptions, cross-worker dependency conflicts or duplicate remediation; a qualifying first-party disturbance-specific response was not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No demonstrated S2-specific discretionary right distinct from the top-level orchestrator&#39;s current work assignment/review acceptance or deterministic Ferment sequence guards. - Decision owner: Not established for S2 at the reviewed boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentManager&#39;s background concurrency cap/queue, GroupJoin, planner-defined chunk order, Ferment&#39;s parallel-cohort/FSM gates and prompt instructions not to work on an agent&#39;s assigned step concurrently. - Closure path: No evidenced cross-S1 conflict → specific coordination decision → changed subsequent S1 behavior loop. Review verdict → Fixer is a current quality-control/audit correction path, not proof of autonomous mediation between colliding operational S1 units. - Boundary reachability: The coordination primitives are shipped and callable; a material S2 conflict mediator is not shown in their active call path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The orchestrator may schedule tasks and interpret reviews, but generic schedule/delegation does not establish the narrower organizational S2 function. Deterministic prevention of illegal simultaneous Ferment steps is support, not discretionary inter-unit conflict ownership. - Evidence: concurrency queue; Ferment next-action sequencer; phase sequencing instructions. - Basis: Bounded negative, not a claim that multi-agent worker collisions cannot arise. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A particular deployment may implement S2 via extra custom agents, external git/worktree coordination or operator conduct; those are not credited to this standard first-party boundary without a concrete closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Control one current coding-job&#39;s global phase selection, budgets/role commitments, correction priority and final completeness after worker and reviewer feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Simple versus complex requests, failing build chunks, disputed review findings, time/token constraints and incomplete current work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The top-level model-backed orchestrator classifies the task, chooses explore/research/plan/build/review phases, allocates work to specified roles/models, decides corrective Fixer calls and stops/reports after bounded verdict cycles. - Decision owner: Kimchi&#39;s current top-level orchestrator model, furnished with Kimchi&#39;s operative system-prompt workflow and Agent tool; it is not the queue/Ferment FSM itself or a human who selected the initial task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: First-party phase/guideline prompts, named persona contracts, default roles, timestamps and reports, optional Ferment state, concurrency/token limits, review-verdict files and stop caps. - Closure path: Current user goal + known workspace/worker state → orchestrator chooses plan and worker responsibilities → Builder/reviewer reports and failures → orchestrator delegates revised Fixer/chunk work or stops with unresolved problems → revised current completion state. - Boundary reachability: Default multi-model mode injects orchestration/agent extensions into ordinary CLI; a model agent actively selects pipeline and revises present commitments. Ferment additionally supplies project-wide scoped steps/recovery but its deterministic scheduler alone is not counted as owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Fixed resource caps enforce choices, while the orchestration model decides which parts of current work need reassignment/correction; remove it and only a passive state machine/agent sessions remain. - Whole-system current view: One installed coding session&#39;s user request, files, plan/spec chunks, active/finished subagents, tests and independent review verdict; not the whole getkimchi company or portfolio. - Current-control decision scope: Current project task phases, which agent/model works which chunk, remediation after findings, bounded retries, completion or transparent halt. - Evidence: classification and phase selection; review remediation/stop policy; phase role decisions; Ferment actual step state. - Basis: Positive source-evidenced agent-owned current control. - Confidence: Medium-high; model compliance is not experimentally validated. - Caveats: Prompt directives can be ignored by a provider model; supported autonomous ownership is structurally reachable, not a measured success rate. Human planning approval remains within a particular operating task, not a proven (P) whole-system parent mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Obtain a second evidence-reading judgment of the current implementation&#39;s source, tests and match to spec, return contradictions to project control without the reviewer making audited source edits. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Builder self-report errors, latent correctness/security defects, unchecked missing functionality, misleading test success and implementation/spec divergence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separately instantiated Reviewer model examines actual code, runs tests/lint, decides APPROVED versus NEEDSFIXES with cited problems, and writes a review report; current controller routes its findings to a Fixer. - Decision owner: The separate Reviewer agent persona and child model session, with a direct read/batch test tool surface independent of the Builder&#39;s final summary. The orchestrator subsequently controls fixes; the deterministic parsing of a verdict is not itself independent judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Read-only-oriented Reviewer persona (read/bash/grep/find/ls plus report write), fresh child context, .kimchi/docs/review.md, no direct source edits in the review contract, feedback routing to Fixer, review/fix round caps. - Closure path: Builder work claims code/test completion → separate Reviewer reads spec and raw source and runs tests/lint → evidence-based findings file → orchestrator reads findings and invokes Fixer → source is corrected or defects are reported to user. - Boundary reachability: The default coding pipeline directs model-backed review delegation, and the shipped Reviewer has direct source/tool capability. The code permits self-review for trivial low-risk changes when the orchestrator is also reviewer; that reduced path is not the independent positive witness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reviewer chooses the substantive discrepancy judgment from firsthand source/tests, rather than copying Builder output or reporting automatic CI alone. Its duty separation is by fresh agent context and role, not necessarily by distinct model family. - Claim being audited: Builder&#39;s assertions that new code matches spec, tests pass and changes contain no material correctness/security defects. - Ordinary reporting path: Builder reports artifact paths and its own build/lint/test outcome to orchestrator. - Complementary access path: Reviewer independently reads changed source and context, can execute checks, and records findings separate from the Builder summary. - Independence boundary: Distinct child session and Reviewer persona restricted by instructions to source-read/report writing, rather than the Builder&#39;s execution context. Shell write prevention is not cryptographically enforced merely by prompt; this is behavioral role independence, not a security isolation guarantee. - Who acts on findings: Top-level orchestrator and separately tasked Fixer, not the reviewer itself. - Evidence: Reviewer direct-source contract; fresh session creation; independent review and return to fix; delegated review conditional. - Basis: Positive conditional independent source-read audit path in standard multi-model code workflow. - Confidence: Medium; independence is meaningful for source access but not guaranteed for every trivial task or provider-run behavior. - Caveats: This does not claim an external institutional auditor, distinct model weights, secure filesystem sandbox or that any sample LLM review actually caught bugs.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Would detect outside/future shifts and autonomously revise the coding organization’s future capabilities and resource premises rather than just execute today&#39;s task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Prospective model ecosystem/library/environment changes and organizational future requirements; no qualifying first-party adaptation loop was established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: None established for external-prospective capability planning. - Decision owner: Not established; provider and maintainers change catalogs/models, while Kimchi records selection, memory and project-scope preferences. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Researcher/web access, memory, auto backend routing pick observation, default-model deprecation fallback, local Ferment memories/decisions and skill installation. - Closure path: Current-job research and saved observations can inform tasks but were not shown to autonomously select a prospective capability strategy and return it to future execution governance. - Boundary reachability: These are shipped first-party extension paths, yet their positive returns stop at routing, task context or configuration repair. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A server-picked concrete model and client-side capability synchronization are not a first-party model agent governing prospective organizational adaptation. - Evidence: backend pick observation; dead-model default repair; Ferment scoped memory. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Operational task research and provider-side future planning may still exist externally; no ownership transferred into the assessed local harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Would adjudicate ultimate organizational mission, identity, invariants and conflicting inside/outside priorities with a binding return to operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Foundational identity and policy conflicts; task authorization and model/permissions choices are a narrower variety class. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party internal or supported parent-mode ultimate identity adjudication and closure evidenced. - Decision owner: Human user owns local task intents, resources and mode configuration, but that is not a proven S5 responsibility at the organization recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Intent boundary instructions, tool permissions, project trust, user questionnaire, phase approvals, settings and hard stops. - Closure path: User constrains or revises a task; no ultimate-policy issue → legitimate authority → binding identity decision → future operating changes loop at the declared organization boundary. - Boundary reachability: These mechanisms are reachable but constrain local task execution rather than governance of Kimchi&#39;s identity/purpose. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: System-prompt invariants, task approvals and static permission gates do not exercise mission/policy discretion simply because an LLM interprets them. - Evidence: task intent boundaries; user-selected mode; parent/worker exclusion tools. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Real users or upstream maintainers may exercise S5 organizationally; their separate private governance is outside the reviewed one-session boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/bb01978d5553f1db429b2dc3c196ee02c0eb9764/assessments/ki/kimchi.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit bb01978d5553f1db429b2dc3c196ee02c0eb9764\">bb01978 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-29T14:24:05Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SAIL-Research-Lab/cheetahclaws\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CheetahClaws</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-29\">2026-09-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SAIL-Research-Lab/cheetahclaws/tree/ec5d091b53c70f6685f1330f509b11c3a51aa214\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ec5d091b53c70f6685f1330f509b11c3a51aa214\">ec5d091 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform user-directed coding/general work through a closed autonomous model/tool loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user requests, repository/file state, tool results, API errors, context pressure, external search results and intermediate execution failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive tool/action or finish response after observing the current conversation and tool results. - Decision owner: the configured model acting as the CheetahClaws agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: active tool profiles, permission checks, quotas, retries, context compaction, parallel-safe dispatch, loop guards and persistent conversation state. - Closure path: request → model decision/tool call → first-party dispatch → tool result appended to history → later model decision → outcome. - Boundary reachability: this is the ordinary shipped cheetahclaws execution loop; no Research Lab/trading module or external child harness is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime code bounds and executes actions, but removing the model leaves no actor choosing materially equivalent substantive next actions. - Evidence: cheetahclaws/agent.py; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider internals are external; the credited autonomy is the first-party closed model/tool feedback path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate destructive filesystem interference between distinct parallel coder operational workcells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two full-loop coder subagents working concurrently in the same repository can overwrite, dirty or otherwise interfere with the same working tree. - Distinct S1 units: independently executing coder subagents, each with its own full CheetahClaws agent loop/history and bounded coding outcome. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent code/file mutations against one shared repository working tree. - Attenuating coordination relation: the model-callable Agent tool exposes isolation=&quot;worktree&quot;, explicitly intended for parallel coding tasks that should not interfere; the runtime creates a separate git worktree/branch and injects its path as the subagent&#39;s execution root. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the isolation decision changes where each subagent&#39;s Read/Write/Edit/Bash-style operations execute for the whole child run, so later actions occur against separate working copies; completion returns branch/worktree-associated results to the parent for later integration. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: credit is not for spawning or messaging workers. It is tied to an explicit parallel-file-mutation conflict and a first-party isolation mechanism whose purpose is to prevent that interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether a parallel coding child should be isolated in its own worktree rather than share the parent&#39;s workspace. - Decision owner: the parent CheetahClaws agent model invoking the model-callable Agent tool and selecting the isolation mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SubAgentManager, git worktree creation/removal, per-child worktreecwd, background threads and result/status tracking. - Closure path: anticipated parallel coding conflict → parent agent selects worktree isolation → runtime creates distinct workspaces/branches → child S1 tool activity is redirected → parent receives separated outcomes for subsequent integration. - Boundary reachability: Agent is a shipped model-callable tool and worktree isolation is implemented by the first-party multi-agent package, not an example-only composition. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: git/worktree machinery enforces separation, while the model owns the discretionary choice to invoke the S2 response for the parallel coding disturbance. - Evidence: cheetahclaws/multiagent/tools.py; cheetahclaws/multiagent/subagent.py; docs/guides/extensions.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: ordinary subagent fan-out, message queues and task dependencies are not independently credited as S2; the positive witness is restricted to the explicit worktree-interference path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party autonomous or constructor path was established for whole-system current control over multiple operational units&#39; shared resources, commitments, priorities or accountability at the reviewed boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established beyond local parent-task decomposition, static budgets/permissions and deterministic workflow progression. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task state, ListAgentTasks, CheckAgentResult, SendMessage, quotas/permissions, Research Lab stage state/budgets and deterministic convergence thresholds provide useful execution control but do not establish the required S3 discretion. - Closure path: not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main agent can delegate, inspect worker status and send follow-ups, but Methodology 0.3.6 does not treat worker selection/delegation/result collection as S3 by itself. Research Lab&#39;s stage transitions are largely predetermined/deterministic workflow control rather than a whole-system resource/commitment bargaining function owned by a supervisor agent. - Evidence: cheetahclaws/task/tools.py; cheetahclaws/multiagent/tools.py; cheetahclaws/research/lab/orchestrator.py; cheetahclaws/research/lab/convergence.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a developer could build a supervisor from generic task/subagent primitives, but general framework expressiveness is below the C threshold.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge a finalized Research Lab operational artifact through a separate post-finalization quality audit and feed the finding back into corrective work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a paper can pass ordinary per-stage producer/reviewer convergence yet remain weak as an integrated final artifact in novelty, rigor, clarity or evidence. - Claim being audited: the quality of the already-finalized report after the ordinary stage graph has completed. - Ordinary reporting path: stage producers create artifacts; ordinary stage reviewers drive producer/revision convergence; finalization writes the report. - Complementary access path: /lab iterate starts after finalization and asks a reviewer pool to score the complete final report independently across four explicit dimensions; documented model assignment prefers heterogeneous reviewer families to reduce shared blind spots. - Independence boundary: the audit is a separate post-finalization pass, uses reviewer roles rather than the producing agent, operates over the integrated final artifact rather than one in-flight stage draft, and can use different model families. It is not credited from the ordinary mandatory stage reviewer loop alone. - Who acts on findings: the reviewer agents supply the dimension scores; first-party meta-loop code aggregates them, selects the weakest dimension, rewinds to a mapped earlier stage and reruns downstream work. In daemon --iterate mode this closes unattended. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independent reviewer agents judge the final artifact&#39;s novelty/rigor/clarity/evidence quality; deterministic routing translates the weakest returned judgment into the corrective stage. - Decision owner: autonomous reviewer-model pool for the audit judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: labiterations history, score parsing/aggregation, dimension-to-stage map, persisted artifacts, resumerun, max/target/plateau/budget guards and backlog daemon. - Closure path: finalized report → independent reviewer scores → weakest dimension → stage rewind → regenerated operational artifacts/report → later iteration re-audits the result. - Boundary reachability: /lab iterate is a shipped command and the bundled daemon automatically invokes it for backlog work queued with --iterate; no development-only evaluator is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime code routes and bounds the audit, but removing the reviewer models removes the substantive quality judgment that determines which dimension is weak. - Evidence: docs/guides/research-lab.md; cheetahclaws/research/lab/iterate.py; cheetahclaws/research/lab/orchestrator.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: reviewers share possible pretrained blind spots, acknowledged by the project. Citation verification alone is not the closure witness because that stage records findings and advances; the positive path is the separate final-artifact audit with targeted rewind.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: adapt the bundled trading workcell&#39;s future decision capability using empirical outcomes from the external market under an explicit operator-owned adaptation decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the trading agents&#39; confidence/recommendation process may be systematically miscalibrated as real market outcomes reveal whether past high/low-confidence calls carried signal. - External distinction: closed paper-trade outcomes and market-relative performance provide empirical evidence about whether prior recommendations actually worked; calibration reports hit-rate/signal quality and the stacker trains on closed trades. - Future / prospective distinction: the documented workflow evaluates historical performance specifically to decide whether to change future recommendation weighting rather than merely explain the completed trade. - Adaptation option generated: train/persist the bundled LightGBM (sklearn fallback) stacker so future confidence can be overridden when historical track record argues against the LLM&#39;s confidence. - Path back into current capability / S3: operator reviews calibration and invokes /trading ml train; first-party code persists stacker.pkl; subsequent trading analyses can use the trained model to change confidence presented to current decision-making. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether empirical calibration is sufficient to train/adopt the learned stacker into future operation. - Decision owner: parent human/operator in the documented trading workflow; no autonomous first-party actor is shown owning the train/adopt decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: paper-trade persistence, calibration metrics, closed-trade feature engineering, ML training/persistence and inference hooks. - Closure path: external trade outcomes → calibration/performance distinction → operator adaptation judgment → /trading ml train → persisted stacker → future recommendation confidence altered. - Boundary reachability: trading is a bundled first-party module with documented commands/storage and a recommended monthly calibration→train workflow; it is not repository-development learning. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: market-facing analysis and BM25 memory are not themselves credited as S4. The evidenced adaptation decision that changes capability remains explicitly operator-triggered, so parent-only notation is appropriate. - Evidence: docs/guides/trading.md; cheetahclaws/modular/trading/PLUGIN.md; cheetahclaws/modular/trading/agents/reflection.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Research Lab literature search is operational research, not credited as S4; generic memory/reflection is not enough. The positive path is limited to the documented external-outcome calibration and parent-triggered capability adaptation.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime path was established for identity/ultimate-policy issues to reach legitimate ultimate authority and return as governing policy for subsequent CheetahClaws operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at identity/ultimate-policy level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission modes, user confirmations, tool profiles, quotas, lab budgets/model overrides, prompts/configuration and trading risk limits constrain operation but do not by themselves supply S5 closure. - Closure path: not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary action approval/configuration and static policy-like limits remain below the S5 threshold; no shipped identity-level escalation/governance loop was found. - Evidence: cheetahclaws/agent.py; docs/guides/research-lab.md; cheetahclaws/modular/trading/PLUGIN.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: users remain ultimate operators in practice, but Methodology 0.3.6 requires a first-party identity/ultimate-policy return loop rather than generic human control.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d9d42892965cd598dace641839c4d605a42fae9f/assessments/cheetahclaws.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d9d42892965cd598dace641839c4d605a42fae9f\">d9d4289 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T19:00:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/e10nMa2k/cc-mini\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">cc-mini</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/e10nMa2k/cc-mini/tree/73c5bc563093b650ea8754bf9101a7507d8328a8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 73c5bc563093b650ea8754bf9101a7507d8328a8\">73c5bc5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce software-task outcomes through autonomous observation/action iteration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown code, tool failures and changing intermediate results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose next tool/action, react to returned output and decide to conclude. - Decision owner: Main model-backed agent and first-party worker agents. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Engine, concrete tools, permission checks, persistence and model transport. - Closure path: Model tooluse → tool execution/result messages → next model decision; no-tool result ends the turn. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model leaves tool transport but removes task-specific action selection; --auto-approve is supported, while default operation constrains writes with user consent. - Evidence: src/core/engine.py; src/tui/app.py; src/features/agents/workermanager.py; src/core/permissions.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: high within the declared source boundary - Caveats: Static source evidence; no live model/provider run or empirical viability claim. - Boundary reachability: src/tui/app.py constructs Engine and first-party tools; workers use the same Engine in the shipped coordinator mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate write interference among concurrently running coding workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two write-heavy workers can modify the same local file set; research can safely overlap. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose which file areas run in parallel, sequence conflicting writes and revise worker instructions. - Decision owner: The model-backed coordinator under its shipped concurrency instructions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent/SendMessage tools, worker task IDs, notifications; no file lock is falsely credited as decision owner. - Closure path: Worker reports return to the coordinator, which synthesizes and sends the next scoped instruction or launches work in a different file area. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The prompt supplies a concrete coordination rule for same-file writes; the agent applies its judgment to task/file overlap, rather than a queue alone deciding. - Evidence: src/features/coordinator.py; src/tui/app.py; src/tools/agent.py; src/features/agents/workermanager.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: high within the declared source boundary - Caveats: Prompt-level coordination is not a hard filesystem reservation or a guarantee that all collisions are prevented. - Boundary reachability: --coordinator wires the concurrency prompt and worker tools into Engine; notification draining calls runquery on that same engine. - Distinct S1 units: Two or more tool-using workers producing research/implementation outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concurrent write-heavy tasks on the same file set risk conflicting edits. - Attenuating coordination relation: Coordinator-directed one-at-a-time work per file set, with safe independent research/verification overlap. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Completion/failure notifications inform synthesized follow-up prompts and worker continuation. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The shipped concurrency instructions explicitly distinguish same-file writes from independent/read-only work; messages carry the chosen adjustment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Regulate the coding team&#39;s current commitments and corrective intervention against the user goal. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Workers fail or pursue an approach that no longer serves the current goal; wrong-direction work consumes present capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Stop misdirected running work, retain or replace failed workers, and issue synthesized corrective specifications. - Decision owner: The model-backed coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Worker status/results/usage, TaskStop abort, SendMessage continuation and worker creation. - Closure path: Current failure or changed-direction evidence reaches coordinator; its stop/restart/replacement decision changes which worker operates and under which instructions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent chooses the corrective commitment intervention from team results; the runtime merely aborts or resumes the chosen worker. This is narrower than budget allocation or organization-wide governance. - Evidence: src/features/coordinator.py; src/features/agents/workermanager.py; src/tools/agent.py; src/tui/app.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: high within the declared source boundary - Caveats: Only the local session/team recursion is credited. Generic user permission and interruption do not independently establish S3=P. - Boundary reachability: The supported coordinator prompt explicitly instructs failure recovery and stopping wrong-direction work; shipped worker tools implement those choices. - Whole-system current view: Coordinator receives worker completion/failure, result and usage across the local team, alongside the user goal. - Current-control decision scope: Present team commitments: continue an existing worker, substitute a fresh one, or stop ongoing wrong-direction work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No complementary audit outside routine production verification established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Worker claims may be wrong; routine checks can miss defects. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying first-party decisive right established for this function. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The cited runtime and documentation are supporting surfaces, not decision owners. - Closure path: No function-specific returned closure at this boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Fresh verifier instructions are ordinary research→implementation→verification workflow; review/simplify runs within normal production. No separately governed sporadic audit path is shipped. - Evidence: src/features/coordinator.py; src/features/skillsbundled.py; src/features/agents/definitions.py; src/features/agents/workermanager.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No external-and-prospective capability adaptation loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User/project information and previous mistakes change across sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying first-party decisive right established for this function. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The cited runtime and documentation are supporting surfaces, not decision owners. - Closure path: No function-specific returned closure at this boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: KAIROS consolidates internal transcripts and preferences; coordinator research and plan mode address the current task. Neither supplies future-environment adaptation options coupled to current team capability. - Evidence: src/features/memory.py; docs/memory.md; src/features/coordinator.py; src/features/plan.py; src/core/config.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No identity/ultimate-policy decision-and-return path established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Permissions, tool risk and plan approval constrain work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying first-party decisive right established for this function. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The cited runtime and documentation are supporting surfaces, not decision owners. - Closure path: No function-specific returned closure at this boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Permission choices, plan acceptance, sandbox configuration and saved preferences govern operational actions; they do not route an identity-policy matter to legitimate ultimate authority. - Evidence: src/core/permissions.py; src/features/plan.py; src/tools/plantools.py; src/core/context.py; src/core/config.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b44ecd935e12f8907c46cf85993cc0793d087629/assessments/cc/cc-mini.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b44ecd935e12f8907c46cf85993cc0793d087629\">b44ecd9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-16T22:40:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/himkt/cafleet\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CAFleet</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-16\">2026-10-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/himkt/cafleet/tree/a991fe8f818a5e65770f32db3867f50b178c2e73\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision a991fe8f818a5e65770f32db3867f50b178c2e73\">a991fe8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: agent members complete assigned coding, tests or code review operations against a real repository via installed scoped role instructions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: requirements ambiguity, code/test errors, tool or source feedback and local decisions about task implementation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: actual model-backed assigned Programmer/Tester/Verifier chooses substantive tool/code/test actions within role constraints and returns results. - Decision owner: external model-backed agent instantiated and role/prompt-scoped through first-party CAFleet skill and member creation; its general model internals are not owned by CAFleet. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Rust CLI member registration/pane creation, embedded skills/presets, durable fleet-scoped message ledger, role-specific task prompt, actual user Git repository/working branch. - Closure path: designated code/test task → first-party member spawn with role prompt → model-backed member interacts with files/tests and adjusts work → persisted cafleet message send result and artifacts → Director sees status. - Boundary reachability: Rust member create with rendered prompt and setup installed roles; supported external Claude/Codex/OpenCode CLIs required as environmental inference executors. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the operating model agent leaves only deterministic broker/pane mechanics and cannot complete coding work. - Evidence: cafleet/src/assets.rs; cafleet/src/cli/member.rs; cafleet/src/broker/members.rs; skills/cafleet-design-doc/execute/execute.md; skills/cafleet-design-doc/execute/roles/programmer.md. - Basis: first-party shipped/invocable composition and real command implementation. - Confidence: medium-high source reachability; no live inference witnessed. - Caveats: bare broker/CLI sessions without model-backed members or design-doc skill cannot inherit these positive functions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent multiple operational monitor cells from concurrently claiming the single live fleet monitoring/wake responsibility. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplicate monitor/wake loops could compete and flood or cross-trigger Director/member attention, confusing sibling operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: hold/reject active monitor registration and stop monitor ticks without the valid owner heartbeat. - Decision owner: first-party Rust/SQLite monitor ownership constructor (not discretionary LLM regulator). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: unique active-monitor index, immediate registration transaction, ownership-checked runtime heartbeat. - Closure path: two would-be monitor member registrations within fleet → unique constraint permits only one active monitor → only owner loop maintains heartbeat and issues batched wake → other candidate fails or stops, leaving unique coordination channel. - Boundary reachability: fleet create bootstraps a monitor and monitortick is the actual running monitor-loop implementation, not merely specification. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic gating of competing monitor operations, distinct from messaging reliability. - Distinct S1 units: model-backed operational monitor members, potentially attempted concurrently; one retained active slot. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two independently initiated monitors both try to wake/support the same live fleet and perturb active worker/Director behavior. - Attenuating coordination relation: transaction-backed exclusive monitor identity and tick ownership heartbeat. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: only the valid monitor supplies Director wake context and all other launched monitor attempts receive explicit errors/stop. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: it excludes physically conflicting current regulatory operations by enforcing exclusive live responsibility. - Evidence: cafleet/migrations/V8uniqueactivemonitor.sql; cafleet/src/broker/members.rs; cafleet/src/monitor/mod.rs; cafleet/src/cli/fleet.rs. - Basis: shipped code, unique constraint and monitor semantics. - Confidence: medium: narrow constrained mode; no observed live monitor collision test claimed beyond source tests. - Caveats: no evidence of automatic cross-member Git conflict suppression or worktree isolation.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: decide and regulate currently pending/blocked fleet work assignments and interventions across the installed design-doc execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unresolved tasks/tests/reviews, failed/spinning members, uneven member availability and user interruptions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model Director decides who to spawn, which current phase/step to release or retry, how to arbitrate defects or escalate, and when work is ready for review. - Decision owner: active model-backed Director using installed cafleet-design-doc skill; broker provides command effects and read-back. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fleet/member list, captures, persisted message send/poll/ack, worker roles, Director rule set, monitor wake. - Closure path: current design-doc/task/member/review state → Director chooses intervention/assignment → real cafleet member create/message send/member ping commands → member actions/new reports/captures return to next Director decision. - Boundary reachability: cafleet setup installs Director/execute skills, and Rust implements the invoked CLI effects; explicit skill invocation required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Director prompt delegates whole-fleet current judgment to an active model, not simple deterministic queued routing. - Whole-system current view: active members, issue/doc state, test results, messages and observer member pane state of one fleet. - Current-control decision scope: membership and task routing, retry/review escalation, work hold, acceptance handoff and shutdown; never tenant-global ultimate policy. - Evidence: skills/cafleet-design-doc/execute/execute.md; skills/cafleet/roles/director.md; cafleet/src/cli/member.rs; cafleet/src/broker/messaging.rs; cafleet/src/monitor/mod.rs. - Basis: installed agent instructions plus executable effects. - Confidence: medium in supported workflow, not empirically observed autonomy performance. - Caveats: Rust broker alone does not choose present commitments; workflow is prompt-compliance dependent.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: separate fresh agent quality review of real code changes against design acceptance with binding rework before operator approval. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect or incomplete implementer claims, missing behavior, defects or code/test mismatch unnoticed by implementers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently inspect the whole branch diff and quality checks, report annotated defects or approved (doc); Director follows mandatory re-review workflow. - Decision owner: a fresh model-backed Reviewer spawned separately after implementation by a model-backed Director, with read-only role constraints; human operator separately controls final publication. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: installed cafleet-design-doc execute Step 5, roles/reviewer.md, Director&#39;s issue-marker protocol, git diff, read-only test tools, message ACK/status, Step 6 human approval. - Closure path: code+tests complete → new distinct Reviewer reads full branch diff and design criteria → Reviewer sends source-line findings → Director dispatches fixes → reviewer rechecks without cap → only approved (doc) permits user acceptance and publication steps. - Boundary reachability: built-in setup skill installation, conditionally invoked execute skill; executable member creation, messaging, prompts and real checkout. Not a default broker-wide transaction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a first-party shipped role binds a genuinely distinct model-backed Reviewer to independent original code evidence; review does not merely repeat implementer self-report. The binding phase sequence is first-party skill instruction rather than hard backend enforcement. - Claim being audited: implementer has delivered requirements-compliant work with passing tests/ready for approval. - Ordinary reporting path: Programmer/Tester results and step completion messages to Director. - Complementary access path: fresh Reviewer reads actual full Git diff, design spec and runs targeted read-only tests, returning explicit source-anchored issues. - Independence boundary: fresh model role has no code authorship/commit permission in the skill and sees diff not just implementer summary; no guarantee of independence across model weights/providers or perfect compliance. - Who acts on findings: model Director sends fixes to Programmer/Tester, repeats separate Reviewer gate and only then asks operator. - Evidence: cafleet/src/assets.rs; skills/cafleet-design-doc/execute/execute.md; skills/cafleet-design-doc/execute/roles/reviewer.md; skills/cafleet-design-doc/reference/coordination.md. - Basis: mode-bounded first-party installed skill autonomous audit return. - Confidence: medium; unobserved reviewer invocation and prompt compliance. - Caveats: not a Rust-enforced hard gate, no global automated quality guarantee, operator may use ordinary CAFleet as generic broker with no execute role.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped fleet-level externally informed prospective organizational capacity adaptation path established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: longer-term changes in coding provider capabilities, tool landscape and work requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide and implement future system adaptations based on external sensing, not evidenced in installed paths. - Decision owner: the human or model can pick member model/roles for current task, but this is deployment/current S3 rather than a separate prospective organ. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model catalog guidance, role presets, cost-mode choice, historical messages/design docs, per-fleet config. - Closure path: current task spawns may select models, but no proven external prospecting → changed future fleet capability → applied new organizational capacity feedback. - Boundary reachability: inspected bundled install, model selection, docs planning and monitor signals; these concern present execution or maintenance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model choosing a cheaper reviewer for this session is not long-horizon S4 future adaptation. - Evidence: skills/cafleet/roles/director.md; skills/cafleet/reference/coding-agents.md; cafleet/src/assets.rs; cafleet/src/monitor/mod.rs. - Basis: bounded negative after checking likely shipped paths. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: humans may create new skills/models outside assessed package.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: distinct legitimate fleet identity/ethos/ultimate policy choice and governing return not demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unresolved organizational purpose, ethical mandate and conflicting long-term versus current demands. - Decisive decision or feedback right: settle ultimate organizational identity commitments and return new policy into the fleet; not evidenced. - Decision owner: operator/user owns publication and higher-authority decisions outside the broker; that is not automatically S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fleet registry/slug and Director identity, model backend choice, role instructions, human approval, command allow/deny/escalation. - Closure path: user approves changes or commands; no distinct ultimate policy issue and authority settlement governing both present and prospective functions. - Boundary reachability: shipped fleet create, identity, deny-command decision surface and Step 6 user approval inspected. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary code approval, permissions and naming do not establish model or parent-owned organizational ultimate identity regulation. - Evidence: skills/cafleet/roles/director.md; skills/cafleet-design-doc/execute/execute.md; cafleet/src/broker/fleets.rs; cafleet/src/cli/fleet.rs. - Basis: bounded negative for assessed recursion. - Confidence: medium-high within assessed mode. - Caveats: real human organizations necessarily have governance outside CAFleet, not credited as first-party S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/fc23ecd690f2c794a50166f730173dff5c54c1fc/assessments/cafleet.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit fc23ecd690f2c794a50166f730173dff5c54c1fc\">fc23ecd &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-02T19:16:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/anYuJia/yode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Yode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-02\">2026-10-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/anYuJia/yode/tree/964d6230afb5e779e3157a452dac1c0ac0d24f16\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 964d6230afb5e779e3157a452dac1c0ac0d24f16\">964d623 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform repository-facing software-engineering work by inspecting code/state, choosing tools, editing files, running commands/tests, and revising actions from returned evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous repository structure, incomplete task requirements, command/test failures, code and git state, provider responses, permissions, context pressure, and changing workspace evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive coding action, tool use, direct edit/command, delegation step, and completion response within the configured authority boundary. - Decision owner: the model-backed Yode AgentEngine actor; delegated sub-agents instantiate the same class of autonomous operational actor at lower recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry; context/session persistence; permissions; cancellation; retry/recovery hints; LSP/search/git/browser/MCP tools; sub-agent runner; desktop event/runtime state. - Closure path: user goal + workspace/context → model decision → first-party tool/sub-agent action → environment result/event → result returns into the model context → next coding decision or completion. - Boundary reachability: the supported desktop runtime directly instantiates this loop; downstream application code is not required to supply the core coding actor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-backed engine leaves execution, persistence and safety machinery but removes the open-ended task-specific coding choices. - Evidence: README.md; crates/yode-core/src/engine.rs; crates/yode-core/src/engine/subagentrunner.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model inference is external; the assessment credits Yode&#39;s first-party role/tool/feedback composition rather than provider internals.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 loop was established at the selected desktop/team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent model-backed team members can in principle contend over shared repository state, but the reviewed standard team/coordinator path does not itself connect that disturbance to an operational attenuation loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2. The main agent may choose dependencies, maxparallel, or explicit worktree isolation, but those are delegation/scheduling/capability choices without a demonstrated shipped relation that detects or structurally maps an actual inter-S1 interference and feeds attenuation back into the affected S1 units. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dependency DAGs, bounded parallel batches, team messaging/state, optional explicit sub-agent isolation: &quot;worktree&quot;, standalone worktree tools, and a WorktreeCoordinator used by the separate multi-repository executor. - Closure path: not applicable at this recursion; no standard desktop/team path was found that takes a concrete inter-S1 conflict/oscillation, applies a coordination relation to it, and returns the result into subsequent behavior of the affected team S1s. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model can decide how to delegate and can explicitly request isolation, but generic sequencing, concurrency limits, messaging and optional isolation capability do not by themselves establish the S2 function. - Evidence: crates/yode-agent/src/planning.rs; crates/yode-tools/src/builtin/coordinator/mod.rs; crates/yode-core/src/engine/subagentrunner.rs; crates/yode-tools/src/worktreecoordinator.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: yode-runtime::multirepo does use WorktreeCoordinator for mutating multi-repository steps, but PRODUCT.md places Multi-repo Agent in P2 and the reviewed supported desktop/team organization does not establish that as its normal inter-S1 coordination path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a current view of the multi-agent coding organization and make task/worker execution decisions over active, ready, blocked, completed and failed work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing worker/task status, dependency readiness, background execution, failures, pending messages, returned workstream results and current orchestration progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the main model-backed agent can choose team/workstream decomposition, dependencies and concurrency, inspect current team state, send new guidance/handoffs, invoke ready-step execution, request/inspect background task output, and choose subsequent current-work interventions. - Decision owner: the main model-backed Yode agent using first-party team/coordinator/task tools. - Whole-system current view: teammonitor and persisted AgentTeamState expose the current team goal/plan plus member counts and member-level planned/running/completed/failed status, ready/blocked/running/completed/failed plan progress, runtime task IDs, result previews and pending team messages. - Current-control decision scope: the main agent can choose/decompose workstreams, dependencies and concurrency, launch or continue current team work through teamrunready / sub-agent tools, send mid-course guidance through sendmessage, inspect current/background results, and create follow-up work in response to failures or completion evidence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentTeamManager; persisted team state/monitor/bundle artifacts; teammonitor; teamrunready; sendmessage; runtime task store; dependency planner; deterministic status reconciliation. - Closure path: current team/task state is exposed to the main agent → the agent interprets current progress/failure/blocked state → selects a current-control action such as dispatching ready work, changing decomposition/concurrency, sending guidance or launching follow-up work → first-party runtime updates worker/team state → the resulting state/output returns for subsequent decisions. - Boundary reachability: team/coordinator tools are part of the shipped desktop agent tool surface and sub-agent runtime; they are not merely an external SDK substrate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic managers expose/enforce state, but removing the main model-backed agent removes the discretionary current-work decisions over what to delegate, how to sequence/parallelize, and how to respond to live results. - Evidence: README.md; crates/yode-tools/src/builtin/coordinator/mod.rs; crates/yode-tools/src/builtin/teamruntime/mod.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the deterministic DAG executor alone is not credited as S3 ownership. The positive classification depends on the main model actor&#39;s access to current team state plus its first-party intervention tools.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge implementation claims and return findings into a path that can block delivery/commit or trigger corrective work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false completion, regressions, missing test coverage, risky assumptions, build/test failures and implementation errors not caught by the implementing S1. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separate model-backed review/verification agent inspects current workspace evidence and produces findings/judgment; that judgment determines whether the review pipeline may proceed to commit absent explicit override. - Decision owner: the dedicated model-backed verification/review sub-agent. - Claim being audited: that the implementation/current workspace changes are correct and safe enough to proceed toward delivery or commit. - Ordinary reporting path: the implementing main/worker S1 returns its own completion text, tool results and team/task result state through the normal coding loop. - Complementary access path: verificationagent, reviewchanges, reviewpipeline, and reviewthencommit launch a separate sub-agent that directly inspects git/workspace state and may run targeted tests/commands with a review-specific tool allowlist. - Independence boundary: the audit actor is a fresh model-backed AgentEngine/sub-agent with a distinct review or verification prompt, conversation context and restricted evidence-gathering tool surface; it is not the implementing S1 merely rereading its own conclusion. - Who acts on findings: the first-party review pipeline deterministically returns a recoverable validation failure and skips commit by default; the parent/main agent receives the findings/error and can correct the implementation and rerun the audit. - Boundary reachability: the dedicated verification/review tools and sub-agent runner are shipped in the first-party yode-tools / yode-core runtime used by the supported desktop composition; no downstream application-authored auditor is required. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated sub-agent runner; restricted inspection/test tool set; persisted review artifacts; findings parsing/counting; deterministic review-pipeline gate; reviewthencommit commit suppression. - Closure path: implementation exists → fresh verification/review sub-agent inspects repository/git/test evidence → findings are returned to the parent/tool pipeline → findings cause a recoverable validation failure and commit is skipped by default → subsequent agent work can address findings before retrying verification/commit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime gate is deterministic support, but the complementary audit judgment is produced by a separate model-backed actor with direct workspace/test access rather than by the implementing S1. - Evidence: crates/yode-tools/src/builtin/verificationagent/mod.rs; crates/yode-tools/src/builtin/reviewpipeline/execution.rs; crates/yode-tools/src/builtin/reviewthencommit/execution.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: confirmation requirements constrain execution authority but do not move the audit judgment away from the independent verification/review actor.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Yode records postmortems, failures, verification evidence and lessons, performs current-run recovery hints, supports repository/web intelligence and can persist memory; these mechanisms do not close a future-oriented adaptation loop that changes organizational capability/strategy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for prospective capability adaptation. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LearningStore; postmortem/lesson persistence; current-run failure/retry hints; repository intelligence/indexes; memory/instruction loading; evaluation artifacts. - Closure path: no standard path was found from external/future distinctions through adaptation-option selection into a persistent change of Yode&#39;s current organizational capability. At the frozen ref, relevantlessons() is defined in the learning store but is not consumed by the operational agent runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: retrospective lesson derivation and present-run recovery can improve continuity/behavior without constituting the required outside-and-future intelligence function. - Evidence: crates/yode-core/src/learning.rs; crates/yode-core/src/engine/intelligenceruntime.rs; crates/yode-core/src/instructions.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: README language such as “Evaluate / Postmortem / Learn” is not promoted to S4 without operational prospective-adaptation closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime identity or ultimate-policy decision loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user, managed, project and local instruction/permission layers constrain tool authority; plan-mode approval and per-tool confirmations constrain execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for an identity/ultimate-policy issue. Managed/user configuration can set stronger operating constraints, but no runtime S5 actor receives an identity-level issue, decides the organization&#39;s governing purpose/policy, and returns that decision into later operation. - Decision owner: none established inside the assessed Yode organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: managed/user/project/local permission layers; project instructions; plan-mode approval; tool confirmation requirements; workspace trust and deny rules. - Closure path: no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate S5 authority → authoritative policy decision → returned governance path was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static or externally edited configuration and permission enforcement define the operating envelope but do not themselves constitute runtime S5 ownership. - Evidence: apps/yode-desktop/src-tauri/src/runtime/turnpermissions.rs; crates/yode-core/src/instructions.rs; crates/yode-tools/src/builtin/planmode/mod.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic approvals and managed policy are important governance/safety mechanisms, but Methodology 0.3.6 does not promote them to S5 without function-specific identity/ultimate-policy closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0039698c28470f5bd69869a7a46a2582ff638021/assessments/yode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0039698c28470f5bd69869a7a46a2582ff638021\">0039698 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-29T00:34:02+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sipyourdrink-ltd/bernstein\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bernstein</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-29\">2026-09-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sipyourdrink-ltd/bernstein/tree/c3d317385a058ae2b5f478399373d09d84d80962\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c3d317385a058ae2b5f478399373d09d84d80962\">c3d3173 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform substantive autonomous task work inside a governed Bernstein run through a supported first-party agent execution mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended coding/research tasks, target-repository state, tool/MCP observations, model outputs, execution failures and returned verification feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: interpret the assigned task and current observations, choose model/tool actions, evaluate returned tool results and continue/revise until an operational result is produced. - Decision owner: the autonomous agent instantiated through Bernstein&#39;s supported openaiagents runner mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bernstein spawner, runner manifest, role/model policy, sandbox provider, MCP bridging, session/lifecycle handling, timeouts, cost tracking, task server and lineage/audit instrumentation. - Closure path: scheduler admits task → Bernstein builds runner manifest and launches first-party openaiagentsrunner → runner constructs SDK Agent with model/tools/sandbox/MCP context → Runner.runsync performs model/tool turns → completion/tool/session events return through Bernstein → task result enters verification and subsequent task operation. - Boundary reachability: openaiagents is a documented built-in Bernstein adapter selectable from normal plan.yaml; the optional SDK extra is an explicitly supported dependency, and the runner/manifest/lifecycle integration is first-party at the frozen revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the autonomous Agent/Runner actor while leaving scheduler, worktrees, claims and gates intact leaves task transport/enforcement but removes the substantive open-ended operational decision loop. External CLI wrappers are not needed for this witness. - Evidence: docs/adapters/openai-agents.md; src/bernstein/adapters/openaiagents.py; src/bernstein/adapters/openaiagentsrunner.py. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: Claude/Codex and similar terminal-agent integrations may place more of the autonomous loop in adjacent CLI products. The A finding relies on the separately documented SDK-backed first-party execution mode, not on inheriting autonomy from every adapter Bernstein can spawn.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concurrent worker interference by preventing overlapping file/task work from being admitted simultaneously and by isolating admitted work in per-agent worktrees. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two or more operational workers may otherwise modify the same or inferred-overlapping repository paths concurrently, producing collision, stale assumptions, conflicting changes or unsafe landing order. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether a candidate task batch is safe to spawn given current file ownership and active worktree modifications, and withhold conflicting work until the disturbance clears. - Decision owner: deterministic Bernstein task-lifecycle/scheduler machinery; no autonomous coordination agent owns the overlap judgment in the standard mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: explicit ownedfiles, inferred affected paths, fileownership, active worktree diff inspection, per-agent worktrees, file locks, merge serialization, dirty-target/read-set/conflict refusal. - Closure path: scheduler considers ready task/batch → first-party lifecycle compares its paths with live-agent ownership and active worktree changes → overlap causes spawn suppression → non-overlapping workers proceed in isolated worktrees → changed ownership/worktree state is reconsidered on later ticks → later S1 admission therefore changes from the coordination result. - Boundary reachability: overlap checks execute in ordinary task claim/spawn lifecycle and worktree isolation is a standard Bernstein execution mechanism, not an example-only constructor. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently runnable autonomous worker tasks instantiated through supported agent execution modes, each with its own task identity/worktree and operational objective. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous edits to the same or inferred-overlapping repository paths can create collision/conflict and invalidate assumptions between independently acting workers. - Attenuating coordination relation: Bernstein withholds a candidate batch when ownedfiles, inferred paths, lock-manager ownership or active worktree diffs overlap, while isolated worktrees and serialized merge paths keep admitted work separated until landing. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a suppressed task is not spawned in that tick; as active ownership/worktree state changes, later scheduling can admit it, changing which autonomous worker acts next. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the witness is a concrete anti-collision relation over plural operational units and shared repository paths, not merely task queues, dependencies or parallelism. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party mechanism establishes the S2 function and closes the attenuation feedback, but the overlap decision is a fixed deterministic policy. No autonomous actor is shown deciding or adapting the coordination relation, so constructor C is published. - Evidence: src/bernstein/core/tasks/tasklifecycle.py; src/bernstein/core/git/incrementalmerge.py. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: dependency ordering, task claiming, shared whiteboard and ordinary message transport are not independently credited as S2. The positive witness is specifically the live overlap/isolation feedback path.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: review the live whole-run task organization and autonomously revise current commitments, roles and priorities when completions, failures or stalls indicate the current operating plan needs correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing task completion/failure state, stalled work, unsuitable role assignment, wrong priority, obsolete commitments, newly necessary tasks and remaining budget pressure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose queue corrections among reassigning role, cancelling work, changing priority and adding a task, based on the current queue and budget/completion/failure context. - Decision owner: first-party LLM-powered ManagerAgent during periodic queue review. - Whole-system current view: the manager review receives the current open, claimed and failed task queue together with completion/failure counters and remaining budget context for the governed run. - Current-control decision scope: role reassignment, task cancellation, priority change and creation of newly necessary work across the live run, with deterministic validation/application by the orchestrator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task-server snapshot, manager prompt/typed QueueReviewResult, completion/failure counters, stall timer, budget tracker, correction validation and deterministic task-server PATCH/POST application. - Closure path: run accumulates three completions, any failure, explicit review request or stall interval → orchestrator instantiates ManagerAgent with current run context → manager reviews open/claimed/failed queue and returns typed corrections → orchestrator validates/applies them to task-server state → deterministic scheduler sees changed roles/priorities/cancellations/new tasks → subsequent worker commitments change. - Boundary reachability: manager queue review is wired into the ordinary orchestrator slow tick and uses shipped thresholds; it is not a documentation-only manager pattern. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the deterministic orchestrator enforces and validates corrections, but the discretionary judgment over which current correction to make is produced by the ManagerAgent. Removing that model judgment leaves counters and enforcement but not the same live organizational correction choice. - Evidence: src/bernstein/core/orchestration/manager.py; src/bernstein/core/orchestration/managermodels.py; src/bernstein/core/orchestration/orchestrator.py. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently inspect completed operational work through a reviewer path that is distinct from the worker&#39;s ordinary production/reporting path and return a corrective judgment before merge. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a worker can report completion while its actual diff contains correctness, security, bug, style or scope defects that ordinary completion claims/signals may miss. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the concrete git diff and task intent using a reviewer model different from the writer, then decide approve versus requestchanges; in blocking mode the latter prevents merge and creates/feeds corrective work. - Decision owner: the autonomous cross-model reviewer (or configured reviewer quorum) invoked by Bernstein&#39;s first-party verifier. - Claim being audited: the worker&#39;s substantive completion claim that its produced repository change correctly satisfies the assigned task and is suitable to merge. - Ordinary reporting path: the worker exits/completes and reports its task result through Bernstein&#39;s normal agent/session completion path, including its own output and completion signals. - Complementary access path: Bernstein independently extracts the concrete git diff from the worker worktree, scoped to task-owned files where declared, and sends that artifact plus task intent to a distinct reviewer model. - Independence boundary: first-party reviewer selection maps writer families to a different reviewer model/provider axis and rejects an explicit reviewer override that collides with the writer model; optional quorum can widen that separation further. - Who acts on findings: Bernstein&#39;s completion/merge-control path acts on requestchanges; with blocking enabled it prevents ordinary merge and routes the finding into corrective/fix work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: diff extraction, explicit writer→different-reviewer mapping, same-model override rejection, optional multi-model voting, conventions injection, blocking configuration, janitor/gate pipeline and corrective task/escalation machinery. - Closure path: worker finishes → Bernstein captures actual worktree diff/task scope → cross-model verifier selects a different reviewer model/provider axis and obtains an independent judgment → requestchanges with blocking enabled prevents normal merge and enters correction/fix handling → later S1 work therefore changes from the audit finding. - Boundary reachability: qualitygates.crossmodel.enabled: true is a documented first-party runtime configuration; the verifier is shipped and integrated into completion processing even though the orchestrator defaults it off. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic tests and janitor checks remain supporting evidence, but the credited audit judgment is made by a separate autonomous reviewer over complementary actual-diff evidence. The writer cannot satisfy this path merely by asserting success. - Evidence: docs/architecture/quality-pipeline.md; src/bernstein/core/quality/crossmodelverifier.py; src/bernstein/core/quality/janitor.py. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: the cross-model verifier is fail-open on some reviewer-call/parse failures and is disabled by default. Those are robustness/default-policy limitations, not absence of the supported autonomous audit mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: turn future/external or trend-relevant evidence about the operating environment into concrete proposals for changing Bernstein&#39;s present routing, model, policy, role-template or provider capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: cost/quality/failure trends and external repository-health regressions can make the current model routing, policy, role templates or provider setup unsuitable for later runs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select an adaptation proposal and admit it into an approval/execution path that can change current capability. - Decision owner: not closed by a first-party autonomous S4 actor in the standard mode; detector/proposal/executor logic is primarily deterministic and configuration/risk driven. - External distinction: when composed with observabilitysnapshotsdir, the detector compares successive external repository-health snapshots and distinguishes new security/coverage regressions from the prior observed environment. - Future / prospective distinction: the detector also treats persistent cost, success-rate and failure-pattern trends as evidence that the current routing/model/template/provider arrangement may be unsuitable for subsequent runs, not merely as a report about the completed run. - Adaptation option generated: typed proposals can change policy, routing rules, model routing, role templates or provider configuration, with explicit risk, expected-improvement and rollback metadata. - Path back into current capability / S3: approved proposals enter the evolution executor, which can apply the configured change (or roll it back on failure), thereby altering the capability/configuration available to later current-operation scheduling and execution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MetricsAggregator, OpportunityDetector, failure-pattern analysis, observability-snapshot regression detection, ProposalGenerator, risk/approval modes, EvolutionCoordinator, upgrade executor, rollback/circuit/invariant machinery. - Closure path: first-party constructor can read task/cost/failure trends and, when explicitly composed with observabilitysnapshotsdir, external security/coverage regressions → detector emits typed ImprovementOpportunity → proposal generator maps it to a concrete policy/routing/model/template/provider change → evolution approval/executor can apply/rollback the change → later operating capability can differ. - Boundary reachability: the evolution package is shipped/exported and its detector explicitly accepts the observability snapshot corpus, but the default AnalysisEngine constructs OpportunityDetector without that external snapshot input; a developer/operator must therefore compose the outside-sensing path and autonomous adaptation authority to close S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the repository supplies a function-specific outside/trend-to-adaptation constructor and execution return path, but its decisive adaptation selection/approval is heuristic/configured rather than autonomously owned. This is narrower than awarding S4 for generic learning, metrics or a roadmap. - Evidence: src/bernstein/evolution/detector.py; src/bernstein/evolution/proposals.py; src/bernstein/evolution/init.py. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: internally observed success/cost metrics alone are not treated as sufficient outside-and-then intelligence. The positive constructor relies on the explicit external observability-regression input plus the shipped adaptation proposal/execution machinery; default wiring remains incomplete.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: establish the authoritative governance playbook that defines forbidden, required and permitted posture for later Bernstein runs, while keeping the ultimate enactment right at the legitimate parent human/operator. - Disturbance / variety regulated: observed governance findings may imply a new desired policy/posture, but a model-generated suggestion must not silently redefine what the governed organization permits or requires. - Decisive decision or feedback right: sign or refuse the exact governance playbook artifact that will govern subsequent runs. - Decision owner: legitimate parent human/operator signing a content-specific governance proposal. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: which governance posture the Bernstein organization will treat as forbidden, required and permitted for future runs, rather than whether one current task should pass a local approval. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: in the claimed parent-governed mode, the human/operator who signs the exact content-addressed governance playbook is the ultimate authority; the drafting model has proposal authority only and cannot enact the policy. - Return-to-operation path: govern apply refuses unsigned drafts, while a human-signed playbook is admitted by hash and is then consumed by later Bernstein runs, so subsequent governed operation executes under the returned parent policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: chain-grounded findings document, model-assisted DraftProposal, canonical content hash, recorded prompt/model/findings digests, proposal status/signature, govern apply refusal for unsigned drafts and run consumption of the signed playbook by hash. - Closure path: first-party governance discovery observes chain-recorded findings → optional model drafts a forbidden/required/permitted playbook proposal → human reviews and signs the exact content hash (or refuses) → unsigned apply is rejected; signed playbook is accepted → later Bernstein runs consume that signed playbook → subsequent operation is governed by the parent decision. - Boundary reachability: ADR-011 is an accepted first-party architectural decision describing the shipped govern discover --assist / DraftProposal / govern apply contract and explicitly states that the resulting signed playbook governs future runs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model may draft policy text but cannot enact it; the repository explicitly makes the human signature the authoritative act and rejects auto-acceptance as a signature. Thus the function is positively closed but parent-owned, yielding P, not A/C. - Evidence: src/bernstein/core/govern/proposal.py; docs/decisions/011-model-drafts-human-signs.md; src/bernstein/cli/commands/governancecmd.py. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: ordinary RBAC checks, budgets, quality gates, task approvals and static bernstein.yaml configuration are not independently credited as S5. The positive witness is specifically the signed future-run governance-playbook authority path.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/bbcea78e47704d914f485ad3cee1d1c080807493/assessments/bernstein.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit bbcea78e47704d914f485ad3cee1d1c080807493\">bbcea78 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T20:55:47Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/pydantic/pydantic-ai-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pydantic AI Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/pydantic/pydantic-ai-harness/tree/434649f87d34aa7e5d48c7bd26ad33c50d57cf10\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 434649f87d34aa7e5d48c7bd26ad33c50d57cf10\">434649f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously execute a bounded coding/research task using first-party Harness tools, workspace/context capabilities and model-driven action selection. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository/web/task ambiguity, file and shell state, tool results, test failures, retrieved evidence, long-horizon context pressure and intermediate errors encountered while completing the task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive investigation/tool/edit/research action and iterate from resulting observations until the assigned task is completed or otherwise concluded. - Decision owner: the model-driven Pydantic AI Agent configured and exported through the Harness&#39;s first-party Coder/Researcher path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Coder/Researcher combined capabilities, filesystem/shell/web tools, repository context, context compaction/output limits, memory, guardrails/spend controls and the Pydantic AI Agent execution substrate. - Closure path: user task → packaged Harness-enabled Agent → model-selected tool/actions → file/shell/web/tool observations → further model decisions → result/changes/evidence. - Boundary reachability: the repository ships a runnable pydanticaiharness.coder:coderagent and documents zero-setup CLI execution; users may also instantiate Agent(..., capabilities=[Coder()]) directly. The assessed actor is therefore wired into a supported first-party Harness mode rather than existing only in examples/dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: first-party capabilities constrain and expose actions, but the autonomous model decides which actions to take from current operational evidence; deterministic tool/runtime machinery does not preselect the task solution. - Evidence: README.md, pydanticaiharness/coder/agent.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Pydantic AI core provides the generic Agent loop as a dependency; this assessment does not import unrelated core metasystem capabilities. S1 credit rests on this repository&#39;s shipped concrete Harness agent/capability composition and documented autonomous operating path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently evidenced S2-specific inter-S1 conflict/oscillation attenuation loop is established in the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no concrete cross-S1 interference witness meeting the Profile threshold is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for an S2-specific attenuation relation. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SubAgents and DynamicWorkflow provide named delegation, isolation, fan-out, chaining, voting, retry control and result transport; budgets bound child execution. - Closure path: no qualifying inter-S1 conflict/oscillation → S2-specific attenuation → returned change in later S1 behaviour loop established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent model can coordinate in the ordinary software sense, but the Methodology requires more than routing/delegation/sequencing/shared results. Parallel alternatives, critic scoring and workflow dependencies do not by themselves prove that distinct S1 units are interfering and that a separate coordination relation attenuates that interference. - Evidence: pydanticaiharness/dynamicworkflow/README.md, README.md. - Basis: explicit absence after function-first review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a downstream workflow can define a real S2 disturbance and relation, but general DynamicWorkflow expressiveness is not enough for a repository-level positive S2 claim.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: in DynamicWorkflow mode, regulate the current whole-team portfolio of subordinate agent calls, dependencies, retries and result-conditioned follow-up commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing intermediate subagent results, failed review outcomes, alternative candidate quality, fan-out resource needs and dependencies that require deciding which child work to launch, retry, chain, select or stop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the workflow program that allocates child-agent calls and conditionally changes subsequent team commitments based on current returned results. - Decision owner: the parent orchestration agent model, which writes the runworkflow Python program from the current task and available subagent catalog. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DynamicWorkflow capability, Monty sandbox, named-agent catalog, isolated child Agent.run calls, asyncio fan-out, result values, hard maxagentcalls and usage/resource limits. - Closure path: current task + available team catalog → parent model writes workflow → child S1 runs return live results → model-authored program branches/selects/retries/dispatches later children → final team output returns to parent operation. - Boundary reachability: DynamicWorkflow is a shipped documented Harness capability. Supplying a catalog of named Pydantic AI agents gives the parent model the standard runworkflow tool; no downstream scheduler/control-plane implementation is required for the autonomous parent to write and execute current-control logic. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the sandbox and budgets deterministically execute/enforce the generated program, but the discretionary whole-team choreography and conditional intervention logic is authored by the parent model for the current task. - Evidence: pydanticaiharness/dynamicworkflow/README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this positive S3 applies to the supported DynamicWorkflow team mode, not every single-agent Coder run. Preconfigured hard budgets and sandbox resource limits are supporting enforcement, not the ownership basis. - Whole-system current view: the generated workflow program holds the set of team members it can call and receives intermediate results from all child runs used in that workflow, allowing later calls/selection/retry to depend on current team outcomes. - Current-control decision scope: allocate/fan out child calls, sequence dependencies, compare/select candidate outputs, retry failed/reviewed work and launch follow-up agents from current intermediate evidence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently review the live operational trajectory with a second model and return corrective steering into the running production agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: instruction drift, unsupported claims, scope creep, hallucination/security concerns and other run-level deviations that may not be detected by the production agent&#39;s ordinary self-report. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge the bounded recent trajectory as AllGood or produce a specific Steer(message) correction. - Decision owner: the separate model-driven TrajectoryJudge agent/model instance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: cadence/window hooks, bounded transcript rendering, concurrent judge evaluation, enforced verdict output type, RunContext.enqueue(..., &#39;asap&#39;), attribution and run usage/failure accounting. - Closure path: live production trajectory → separate judge receives raw recent transcript/tool evidence → independent AllGood/Steer verdict → Steer enqueued to production conversation → next model request receives correction → subsequent production behaviour changes. - Boundary reachability: TrajectoryJudge is a shipped documented capability that can be added directly to an Agent with its own model/instructions or a full distinct judge Agent; the steering enqueue path is supplied by the capability itself. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit judgment is made by a second autonomous model rather than by deterministic validation or the same production actor; the runtime only schedules the review and transports the verdict. - Evidence: pydanticaiharness/trajectoryjudge/README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the judge sees a rendered recent trajectory rather than independently executing the environment itself; the complementary path is direct access to user/assistant/tool-call/tool-result evidence rather than relying on the production agent&#39;s final summary. - Claim being audited: the live agent run remains on-task, evidence-grounded and otherwise compliant with the judge&#39;s specified concern. - Ordinary reporting path: the production agent&#39;s own messages, final output and normal task/tool loop. - Complementary access path: the judge receives a bounded raw transcript including user messages, assistant messages, tool calls and tool results on an independent cadence while the run is still active. - Independence boundary: a separate model or full Agent instance owns its own instructions/model configuration and cannot be replaced by the production agent&#39;s self-evaluation without removing the credited judge actor. - Who acts on findings: the Harness capability enqueues Steer into the running production conversation, and the production agent receives it on the next model request and changes subsequent action accordingly.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: expose a first-party path by which an agent can detect a missing host behavior during operational work, generate and validate a new reusable capability, and persist that adaptation for future activation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task/environment requirements reveal that the current host lacks a needed guardrail, instruction, tool or lifecycle hook that would improve future operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide what new capability to author and generate its Python implementation from the observed need; activation of that authored capability in later operation still requires host/orchestrator composition. - Decision owner: the running agent owns adaptation-option generation; the complete future-capability closure is not autonomous because the surrounding orchestrator/application must load and pass active authored capabilities into later runs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: authorcapability, import/construction/static-getter validation, persistent source files + manifest.json, listing/disabling and store.loadactive(). - Closure path: current task exposes missing host behavior → agent authors/validates/persists capability → active capability store records option → documented host integration loads active capability into a subsequent agent.run → future capability changes. The final activation edge requires composition, which is why state is C rather than A. - Boundary reachability: CapabilityCreation is a shipped Harness capability with default agent-facing guidance and persistence/validation machinery. The S4-specific option-generation path is first-party; only the documented next-run activation loop remains for the developer/orchestrator to compose. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: option discovery/authorship is agent-owned, but the library explicitly states that capabilities cannot enter the live run and that the orchestrator owns the integration contract for loading them on subsequent runs; therefore autonomous S4 closure is incomplete. - Evidence: pydanticaiharness/capabilitycreation/README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream application that implements the documented load-active loop may close autonomous S4 for that separate composed system-in-focus, but that composition is not credited back to this repository assessment. - External distinction: during a coding/operational task the agent discovers that the host lacks a needed behavior such as a guardrail, tool, instruction or hook. - Future / prospective distinction: authored capabilities cannot affect the already-running execution; they are explicitly intended for activation on the next run or a later loop iteration. - Adaptation option generated: a validated persistent AbstractCapability implementation authored by the agent and recorded in the capability store/manifest. - Path back into current capability / S3: store.loadactive() returns persisted active capabilities for the host to pass into a later agent.run; this is a function-specific return path but requires the host/orchestrator integration edge to close.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity / ultimate-policy decision loop is established at the reviewed agent-organization recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying identity/ultimate-policy disturbance established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system instructions, guardrails, spend/tool limits, tool approval, permission/configuration and capability composition constrain behaviour but do not constitute ultimate-policy ownership. - Closure path: no identity/policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned governance of subsequent organization loop established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-owned task planning/workflow control and self-extension remain operational/current-control/adaptation functions. They do not show the agent deciding the organization&#39;s ultimate identity/policy, and user-authored constraints do not become S5 merely because they are authoritative configuration. - Evidence: README.md, pydanticaiharness/capabilitycreation/README.md. - Basis: explicit absence after boundary review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: tool approval and operator configuration can represent legitimate human authority over particular actions without establishing a complete parent S5 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9828806eeec61c022133c2a70e42890196b90300/assessments/pydantic-ai-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9828806eeec61c022133c2a70e42890196b90300\">9828806 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-04T02:45:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SafeAI-Lab-X/ClawKeeper\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ClawKeeper</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-04\">2026-10-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SafeAI-Lab-X/ClawKeeper/tree/69db077317bd8a89777c7670295539ed93145f46\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 69db077317bd8a89777c7670295539ed93145f46\">69db077 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: make a contextual safety decision for a proposed tool action and close that decision into the host execution boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: benign versus malicious or drifted shell/filesystem/network/tool actions, prompt-injection consequences, credential access/exfiltration chains, contextual false positives and bypasses that require intent/history-sensitive judgment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose allow, ask, or deny for the proposed action after interpreting current intent, recent trajectory and deterministic evidence. - Decision owner: the model-backed Watcher role in the enabled Watcher mode; deterministic guards/post-filter constrain and can override that decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic guard chain; Watcher prompt; post-filter; session history; host adapter; pre-tool hook; HTTP Watcher client. - Closure path: host proposes tool call → deterministic findings + intent/history reach Watcher → model-backed safety proposal → deterministic post-filter → adapter returns block/allow result → host skips or proceeds with the tool operation. - Boundary reachability: the Watcher daemon/client and host adapter are shipped first-party integration surfaces; downstream users configure a model endpoint but do not need to invent the safety role or enforcement feedback path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the Watcher model, deterministic guards still enforce known patterns but cannot make the same contextual judgment over intent and recent trajectory. The model therefore owns the open-ended safety discretion in this first-party mode. - Evidence: clawkeepercore/watcher/agent.py; clawkeepercore/watcher/tools.py; clawkeepercore/watcher/policy.py; clawkeepercore/adapters/hermes.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: host-agent task reasoning is not credited. S1 is ClawKeeper&#39;s own safety operation, not the external coding operation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material same-recursion inter-S1 coordination function was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple guard modules and external tool calls coexist, but they are stages/inputs in one safety path rather than distinct autonomous S1 units whose interaction produces an evidenced interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S2. - Decision owner: not established as a first-party S2 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ordered guard chains; deterministic strictest-wins post-filter; per-session history; adapter routing. - Closure path: guard findings are combined inside one safety decision path; no separate inter-S1 conflict→coordination→changed S1-behavior loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: aggregation, routing and arbitration among guard outputs do not by themselves establish S2. - Evidence: clawkeepercore/watcher/policy.py; clawkeepercore/watcher/tools.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: coordination among external agents protected by one deployment would belong to that host organization unless independently closed by ClawKeeper.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: supervise the current protected agent trajectory as a whole and intervene in the next tool action when its relation to intent/history or current risk becomes unsafe. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multi-step drift, attack chains that become suspicious only across recent calls, mismatch between stated intent and proposed action, current deterministic findings and accumulated session context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: at each proposed tool call, use session-level intent and recent-call state to permit the current trajectory to continue, require escalation, or halt the action before execution. - Decision owner: the model-backed Watcher role, subject to stricter deterministic post-filter vetoes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bounded per-session history; remembered intent; deterministic findings; Watcher prompt; fail-safe; post-filter; blocking pre-tool hook. - Closure path: current session intent + recent tool history + proposed next action → Watcher whole-session safety judgment → deny/ask/allow → host pre-tool enforcement changes the next operation → the call is appended to session history for the following current-control decision. - Boundary reachability: Watcher history, evaluator, client and Hermes pretoolcall enforcement are shipped and wired as a supported optional ClawKeeper mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic rules can veto known hardline cases, but contextual current-trajectory intervention depends on model judgment over the whole recent session. Removing the model removes that discretionary supervisory choice. - Evidence: clawkeepercore/watcher/agent.py; clawkeepercore/watcher/tools.py; clawkeepercore/adapters/hermes.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is safety current-control over the protected session, not task prioritization for the external host agent. - Whole-system current view: stated intent, bounded recent tool-call trajectory, proposed next call and current deterministic guard findings for the protected session. - Current-control decision scope: whether the session may execute the proposed next tool action, must ask/escalate, or must block it now.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: on-demand audit and scanners provide materially different evidence, but no standard first-party feedback path automatically or authoritatively closes those findings back into current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: configuration weaknesses, dangerous skills, historical log patterns and control-catalogue findings can be detected outside the routine pre-tool path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as a closed S3 audit judgment that necessarily returns into subsequent current regulation. - Decision owner: audit findings are deterministic; remediation requires a separate operator/client invocation of maintenance or manual action. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: /v1/audit; log/skill scanners; finding scores; harden; rollback; next-step recommendations. - Closure path: audit/scanner reads independent state/history → returns findings/recommendations; any transition into harden or manual remediation is a separate external action, so the required findings→control closure is not first-party-complete. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the evidence path is complementary, but observability plus an optional separate remediation API is insufficient to credit a closed S3 function. - Evidence: clawkeepercore/audit.py; clawkeepercore/scanner.py; clawkeepercore/maintenance.py; clawkeepercore/server.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a host/operator composition can close this loop; that external composition is not credited to ClawKeeper alone.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: learn a new future-facing deterministic guard from an observed external attack/bypass pattern and return it into live safety capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: novel malicious commands or close variants that evade the existing deterministic guard repertoire but are recognized contextually by the Watcher. - Decisive decision or feedback right: generalize a confidently detected bypass into a candidate regex/guard target and decide whether it is specific enough to become a persistent future guard. - Decision owner: the model-backed Watcher synthesis role generates the adaptation; deterministic validation rejects invalid, duplicate or benign-overmatching candidates before activation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: high-confidence missed-guard trigger; synthesis prompt; benign corpus; regex compilation; LearnedPatternStore; guard-target classification; hot-reload bridge; optional upstream fingerprint sharing. - Closure path: external proposed call bypasses deterministic guards → Watcher denies/asks with high confidence → model synthesizes a generalized pattern → validation/persistence accepts it → applylearnedpatterns() mutates live guard rules → future calls are regulated by the new capability. - Boundary reachability: learning, persistence and hot reload are called directly by the shipped Watcher evaluator when the trigger condition holds; no downstream glue is required beyond enabling Watcher mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic memory is not credited; the positive witness is the model&#39;s prospective generalization of a newly observed threat into a reusable guard option that is automatically returned to present capability. - Evidence: clawkeepercore/watcher/agent.py; clawkeepercore/watcher/learner.py; clawkeepercore/watcher/reload.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: optional upstream sharing is not needed for the local S4 closure; the credited loop is local learning from externally presented attack variety. - External distinction: an action that passed all current deterministic guards but is judged dangerous by the contextual Watcher. - Future / prospective distinction: the synthesized regex is explicitly required to match the observed command and close variants, so it is a guard for future related actions rather than a record of the single episode. - Adaptation option generated: a model-generated candidate pattern plus target guard class and confidence, bounded by benign-corpus and regex validation. - Path back into current capability / S3: accepted candidates are persisted and immediately hot-reloaded into live deterministic guard modules used by subsequent pre-tool safety decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material identity/ultimate-policy closure was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: safety policy, confirmation thresholds, guard rules and fail-safe behavior constrain execution but remain ordinary operational safety policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an identity- or ultimate-policy-level issue. - Decision owner: configuration/operator for policy values; Watcher and deterministic guards apply those values within current safety operation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: default policy; deterministic hardline vetoes; config; guard rules; operator confirmation path. - Closure path: not applicable at S5. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a safety filter or policy file does not become S5 merely because it has final veto power over ordinary tool calls. - Evidence: clawkeepercore/judge.py; clawkeepercore/watcher/policy.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a parent organization may treat some security policy as identity-level governance; that higher-recursion authority is not a first-party ClawKeeper function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a070822fa4ce9b9dc6fda1c62a7c3cb6a37697e2/assessments/clawkeeper.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a070822fa4ce9b9dc6fda1c62a7c3cb6a37697e2\">a070822 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-17T13:04:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SethGammon/Citadel\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Citadel</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-17\">2026-10-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SethGammon/Citadel/tree/38f3a4e7fa13cd48cfbedde872778b847af6c842\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 38f3a4e7fa13cd48cfbedde872778b847af6c842\">38f3a4e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute coding, research, review or test tasks in the target repository through first-party scoped agent roles and skills. - Disturbance / variety regulated: variable user requirements, actual repository state, tool errors and test results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the configured coding model chooses and revises substantive code/research/tool steps to fulfill each bounded task. - Decision owner: provider-backed agent operating bundled Citadel skills/prompts; provider internal inference and generic vendor tools are external. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: /do routing, installed skill definitions, delegate assignments, Git worktree/checkpoint state, evidence and callbacks. - Closure path: user outcome → route to model-backed specialist or delegate → tool/source/test work → returned result and observed evidence → correction and delivery. - Boundary reachability: the declared pinned /do and /archon instructions are shipped plugin skills; model executor is separately installed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model, scripts can route/validate but do not solve arbitrary engineering work. - Evidence: skills/do/SKILL.md; skills/archon/SKILL.md; skills/fleet/SKILL.md; skills/review/SKILL.md - Basis: direct installed skill invocation and source code -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concurrent inter-agent interference by detecting overlapping writable scopes and holding a competing work claim. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous operational agents editing the same module/worktree or conflicting merge ownership. - Decisive decision or feedback right: allow a first-party scope claim only when findOverlap returns no existing colliding writable scope, otherwise throw SCOPEOVERLAP. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic coordination constructor; assignment choices still belong to model orchestrator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: claim registry, prefix-overlap checks, read-only exemptions, task/worktree isolation and workflow claim/release instructions. - Closure path: agents choose bounded scopes → claimScope compares active write owners → overlap aborts competing claim/feedback → orchestrator must re-scope/serialize work; release restores opportunity. - Boundary reachability: core claims module is source-reachable via project-local coordination tooling and shipped /archon/fleet steps rather than merely in a document. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: conflict resolution is a deterministic constructor, not independently model-owned S2, and no global lock guarantee follows. - Evidence: core/coordination/claims.js; core/coordination/instances.js; skills/archon/SKILL.md; skills/fleet/SKILL.md - Basis: source-structural explicit collision mechanism - Confidence: medium, with non-atomic cross-process race caveat - Caveats: the lookup/write sequence has a TOCTOU race; this is bounded mitigation not complete exclusion. - Distinct S1 units: two or more concurrent model-backed fleet/campaign delegates with declared writable project paths. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing work on overlapping source paths can overwrite or invalidate sibling changes. - Attenuating coordination relation: scope-overlap comparison and refusal before writing the new ownership claim; optional isolated worktrees. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: on SCOPEOVERLAP, competing work is held/re-scoped instead of legitimately continuing under the claimed scope. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: checks a material competing-write relation between operational agents and returns a refusal rather than just recording messages or tasks.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: current allocation, accountability, intervention and recovery across the scoped Citadel campaign&#39;s operational tasks and delegates. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled phases, failed verifications, scope collisions, cost/resource constraints and changing evidence about current delivery. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model Archon decides campaign phase order, delegate assignments, direction corrections, retries and escalation based on whole-campaign ledger and evidence. - Decision owner: model-backed Archon current controller; deterministic scripts/checkpoints/evidence tests are enforcement or support. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: campaign/feature/decision logs, context handoff, scope-claims, phase validator returns, command/checkpoint/human gates. - Closure path: campaign state and active phase results → Archon model chooses intervention/redelegate/hold/advance → updates campaign and delegate action → subsequent verifier/status feedback. - Boundary reachability: explicit supported /archon skills instruct complete campaign read, phase choice, delegation, corrective and completion decisions, not just scheduling. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: if the Archon model is conceptually removed, the state/gates cannot select revised goals, allocate priorities or choose rework among alternatives. - Evidence: skills/archon/SKILL.md; skills/do/SKILL.md; core/coordination/claims.js; agents/phase-validator.md - Basis: first-party model-led shipped campaign mode - Confidence: medium-high for source-described model authority - Caveats: no separate parent-governed S3 mode credited merely because user can approve a blocking step. - Whole-system current view: active campaign, phase end conditions, feature/decision ledger, delegation/claim context, blockers and verification evidence. - Current-control decision scope: choose work owners/phase resources, rework, interruption, corrections, failure escalation and delivery closure.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: complementary independently judged audit of operational producer claims using direct reality evidence rather than re-parsing ordinary handoffs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: producer HANDOFF claims may be inconsistent with changed code or actual behavior. - Decisive decision or feedback right: distinct direct-artifact reviewer could return findings to compel new producer work, but entire function-specific independently owned path is not proven. - Decision owner: unresolved: phase-validator checks handoff claims, read-only arch-reviewer can examine files and /review independently reads diff; their binding integration into campaign rework is not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dedicated reviewer persona, five-pass review skill, phase-end evidence runner and phase validator. - Closure path: plausible independent reviewer real file/diff inspection → finding → Archon action/rework; inspected default requirement instead validates HANDOFF plus ordinary test gates. - Boundary reachability: all reviewer artifacts and /archon are packaged at exact source, yet distinct reviewer invocation and audit-specific correction path are not proven in a supported completed mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: read-only role designation or test gate is insufficient to declare a separate audit authority. - Evidence: agents/arch-reviewer.md; skills/review/SKILL.md; skills/archon/SKILL.md; core/verification/profiles.js - Basis: plausible in-boundary pathway without decisive independent closure - Confidence: medium confidence in explicit uncertainty - Caveats: do not infer mandatory external reviewer, S3 autonomy, or independent quality from phase validator alone. - Claim being audited: a phase&#39;s implementation and evidence really meet architectural, code/test and requested constraints. - Ordinary reporting path: delegate HANDOFF/phase completion and verification status to Archon. - Complementary access path: separate read-only reviewer can inspect actual source and changes; not proven tied to every qualifying phase. - Independence boundary: dedicated read-only reviewer is distinct as a persona but first-party review-to-rework call path cannot yet be established. - Who acts on findings: Archon can re-delegate remediation in general, but whether findings from a distinct audit alter phase advancement is unproven.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: explicit prospective adaptation of skill capabilities to future task/environment requirements using measured feedback and model-led exploration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new/failing performance criteria, changed repository constraints, skill drift, unfamiliar target scores and recurring usability shortcomings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in opt-in /evolve mode, model director generates hypotheses/options, chooses improvement attack axes, applies tested nonregressing skill edits and cross-pollinates confirmed patterns. - Decision owner: configured model director running shipped /evolve skill; operator sets target and approved rubric, exceptional rubric &#39;level-up&#39; returns to parent but no distinct closed parent S4 mode claimed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scoring/rubrics, scouting agents, isolated worktree experiments, belief model, evidence logs, verification oracle, commit/reload-skills and /learn pattern compilation. - Closure path: observed external/task scores → prospective hypothesis → independent scout → model selects future skill edit → tested modification/commit → reload or future session uses changed skill, with repeated reassessment. - Boundary reachability: shipped /evolve is user-invocable and explicitly names Phase 5 modification + Phase 6 evidence compilation + Phase 7 cross-skill application; requires approved rubric and configured provider. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model autonomously selects and applies evidence-backed future skill changes within its declared constraints; a static wiki or generator alone would not do this. - Evidence: skills/evolve/SKILL.md; skills/learn/SKILL.md; skills/do/SKILL.md - Basis: explicit skill-defined source-reachable prospective action loop - Confidence: medium; no witnessed trials or measured improvement - Caveats: this is conditional opt-in skill adaptation rather than default project control or automatically evolving the external model; rubric choice remains human prior policy. - External distinction: skill/target quality feedback from tasks and evaluation inputs plus actual test/regression results, not simply next action on current task. - Future / prospective distinction: updates stored skill behavior for future independent tasks and sibling skills, with pattern library cross-pollination. - Adaptation option generated: hypothesis-driven skill patch, verified axis change or transferable pattern from observed scores. - Path back into current capability / S3: verified edits to SKILL.md are committed and reloaded where supported or applied in the next model session, changing future task routing/behavior.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct ultimate organizational ethos/identity settlement path proven for the local project Citadel campaign. - Disturbance / variety regulated: fundamental project/fleet identity and normative tensions between current operations and adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: legitimate ultimate policy right and normative return to mission operations would be required; no separate material path identified. - Decision owner: human user chooses installation, project policy and approves risk/level-up; the model is not ultimate identity authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project-local configuration, hook permissions, risk governance, rubrics and controlled user decisions. - Closure path: permissions, checkpoints and human approval govern task/skill changes but do not constitute independent first-party whole-system ultimate-policy closure. - Boundary reachability: inspected /do/archon/evolve policy, project configuration, risk/manual gates and role prompts at pinned ref; no external organization borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary approval of risky actions and metric rubric edits is not S5, and no first-party S5 autonomous loop found. - Evidence: skills/archon/SKILL.md; skills/evolve/SKILL.md; core/config/identity.js; skills/do/SKILL.md; docs/OPERATIONCONTROL.md - Basis: bounded negative at local project operation boundary -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/48c335b7bb4ff17d555163ebb83764b8d1a42a37/assessments/citadel.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 48c335b7bb4ff17d555163ebb83764b8d1a42a37\">48c335b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T23:19:11+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/scion\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Scion (Google Cloud)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-24\">2026-09-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/scion/tree/bdf5b6d135bb7585e242d2f08f4c54b3de2c2940\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision bdf5b6d135bb7585e242d2f08f4c54b3de2c2940\">bdf5b6d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform bounded project work through an autonomous LLM/harness loop inside a Scion-managed identity, workspace and tool environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous tasks, repository/workspace state, tool results, model observations, command failures and domain-specific uncertainty encountered while producing the assigned outcome. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose task-local reasoning, actions/tools and follow-up behavior needed to complete the assigned work within the selected harness and template constraints. - Decision owner: the model-driven runtime agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Scion container/runtime provisioning, templates, harness-config resolution, credentials/secrets, workspace mounts, project identity, platform skills, limits and Hub/Runtime-Broker lifecycle plumbing. - Closure path: user/orchestrator assigns a task → Scion provisions the agent → the model/harness chooses actions and consumes observations in its own loop → produced artifact/result returns to the user/orchestrator/project. - Boundary reachability: scion start and the documented local/Hub modes provision supported harness agents directly; platform skills and workspace context are injected during standard provisioning rather than requiring repository-development wiring. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Scion controls execution substrate and boundaries, while the substantive task-local reasoning/action choices belong to the model-driven agent process. - Evidence: README.md; docs-site/src/content/docs/concepts.md; docs-site/src/content/docs/reference/agent-config.md; resources/platformskills/scion-agent-manage/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: underlying harness/model software is external. The ownership claim concerns the autonomous agent Scion provisions and governs through its first-party runtime boundary.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate destructive workspace/filesystem interference among distinct concurrently operating project agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: sibling agents working against the same project can collide through shared working trees, branches, files, state or credentials, producing conflicting edits or cross-contamination. - Decisive decision or feedback right: place agent executions into a workspace-sharing regime that isolates mutable work per agent and carry later Git reconciliation through the isolated branch/worktree context. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic/project-configured workspace provisioning; no autonomous S2 actor is established for the decisive isolation choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: worktree-per-agent and clone-per-agent sharing modes, per-agent branches, dedicated home/state, shadow mounts, project-scoped identity and the injected git-sandbox conflict-resolution protocol. - Closure path: multiple S1 agents are admitted to one project → Scion provisions separate worktrees/clones/branches → subsequent edits occur on isolated work surfaces rather than one mutable checkout → later merge/rebase reconciliation resolves any remaining Git conflict before integration. - Boundary reachability: worktree-per-agent is documented for local Git projects and clone-per-agent for Hub-managed Git projects; the provisioning code creates the per-agent worktree/branch and git-sandbox is conditionally injected for Git workspaces in the shipped runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the S2-specific attenuation relation is first-party and operational, but workspace isolation is selected/enforced by project/runtime policy rather than an autonomous agent deciding how to coordinate competing S1 units. The agent-side Git skill supports reconciliation but does not transfer ownership of the isolation decision. - Evidence: docs-site/src/content/docs/concepts.md; docs-site/src/content/docs/local/workspace.md; pkg/agent/provision.go; resources/platformskills/git-sandbox/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: shared-plain intentionally permits a non-isolated mode, so the credited S2 path is the supported Git-isolation modes rather than a claim that every Scion project always receives this attenuation. - Distinct S1 units: two or more independently running project agents, each with its own identity and task outcome. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent access to one repository/workspace can create overlapping edits, branch/worktree collisions or cross-contamination of mutable project state. - Attenuating coordination relation: worktree-per-agent/clone-per-agent provisioning separates working state and branch context for each S1 agent; shadow/per-agent state isolation further prevents sibling access to agent-local state. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: each agent&#39;s later file and Git operations are constrained to its provisioned worktree/clone/branch; integration occurs only through a later merge/rebase step rather than uncontrolled shared writes. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: Scion explicitly ties the workspace-sharing modes and isolation machinery to preventing interference/conflicts between concurrent agents, rather than merely moving messages or assigning tasks.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current project/team portfolio of agents and commitments by inspecting live agent state and intervening in worker lifecycle, recovery, timing and cleanup. - Disturbance / variety regulated: completed, blocked, stalled, crashed or limits-exceeded agents; unanswered human questions; concurrent-agent load; failed starts; stuck sessions; workers whose artifacts are not yet safely preserved; and timed work that must survive individual worker sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which workers to create, inspect, continue, interrupt, recover, recreate, stop or delete; verify that owed artifacts exist before cleanup; and route recurring/timed work through a long-lived orchestrator. - Decision owner: the model-driven team orchestrator/creator agent in the standard agent-management path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scion list, scion look, logs, ancestry-based descendant management rights, lifecycle state machine, messaging, scheduler, Runtime Broker and Hub state persistence. - Closure path: current project/worker state becomes visible to the orchestrator → the agent diagnoses current need/state → it issues create/message/recovery/stop/delete/schedule actions → Scion enforces the transition → the changed project portfolio is visible for the next control decision. - Boundary reachability: platform skills are injected by Scion provisioning; team-creation defines an orchestrator that creates/manages workers; scion-agent-manage exposes project-agent inspection/lifecycle operations; scion-scheduler explicitly directs timed agent creation through a long-lived orchestrator so lifecycle remains owned by a reasoning actor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Hub/runtime deterministically records and executes lifecycle transitions, but the choice among recovery, continuation, recreation, cleanup and new work is made by the autonomous orchestrator from current project state. - Evidence: resources/platformskills/team-creation/SKILL.md; resources/platformskills/scion-agent-manage/SKILL.md; resources/platformskills/scion-agent-manage/references/troubleshooting.md; resources/platformskills/scion-agent-manage/references/agent-lifecycle.md; resources/platformskills/scion-scheduler/SKILL.md; docs-site/src/content/docs/concepts.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: deletion of a project initiator/lead is intentionally reserved to explicit human instruction. That is a bounded parent constraint on agent S3 authority, not by itself a separately evidenced complete parent-governed S3 mode. - Whole-system current view: within the declared project/team recursion, the orchestrator can list the active project agents, inspect individual phase/activity/detail and logs, receive child state-change notifications and inspect project-scoped schedules/history. - Current-control decision scope: worker admission/creation, lifecycle and recovery, current task-agent continuity, cleanup/resource release, timed callbacks/recurring work and management of the orchestrator&#39;s descendant hierarchy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no standard-distribution complementary audit function is established at the declared product boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as a first-party S3 loop; ordinary worker self-report or producer output can be inspected/reviewed, but Scion does not itself wire a sufficiently independent auditor with a distinct access path and corrective control return. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: logs, telemetry, scion look, arbitrary reviewer/security-auditor templates and team-creation review-cycle composition can support an audit organization but do not themselves supply its independence or closure. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Boundary reachability: no qualifying built-in reviewer/auditor path was found in the injected platform skills or documented standard team/runtime contract. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a user can construct a reviewer role, and Scion&#39;s own repository contains development-time independent reviews, but generic role/template creation plus same-repository dogfood does not establish an S3-specific product path. - Evidence: resources/platformskills/team-creation/SKILL.md; docs-site/src/content/docs/concepts.md; resources/platformskills/scion-agent-manage/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this does not claim Scion cannot host an excellent independent reviewer; it means the reviewed standard distribution does not itself close or specifically construct that organizational independence. - Claim being audited: not fixed by the standard distribution. - Ordinary reporting path: workers return artifacts/results and expose normal state/logs to their creator/orchestrator. - Complementary access path: not established as a built-in independent path; custom reviewer templates can be created but their access/independence is adopter-defined. - Independence boundary: not established in the standard operating contract. - Who acts on findings: not established by a standard S3 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: redesign the agent organization&#39;s future role/workflow/capability structure in response to new or changed project/user requirements. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a task/domain may require new specialist roles, changed collaboration topology, a different sequential/parallel/debate/review workflow, new reusable skills or a restructuring of an existing team. - Decisive decision or feedback right: infer required roles and workflow from a high-level description, choose the orchestrator/workers and communication pattern, and create or modify the templates/skills that determine later team behavior. - Decision owner: the autonomous agent using the shipped team-creation platform skill. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: .scion/templates/ format, scion-agent.yaml, role instructions/system prompts, local or registry skill references, default-template inheritance and standard scion start --type execution. - Closure path: a new external/project requirement is presented → the agent inspects existing roles/workflow when applicable → it generates adaptation options and writes revised/new team templates → a subsequent orchestrator/worker run uses those templates → current organizational capability changes. - Boundary reachability: team-creation is embedded in resources/platformskills/ and platform skills are injected during normal agent provisioning; the resulting templates are the documented first-party mechanism for starting specialized agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the user may initiate the request and bound the objective, but the skill explicitly gives the model agent the analysis/design work of identifying roles, workflow and orchestration and turning that judgment into executable team configuration. - Evidence: resources/platformskills/team-creation/SKILL.md; pkg/agent/provision.go; docs-site/src/content/docs/reference/agent-config.md; docs-site/src/content/docs/concepts.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this is an agent-owned, explicitly invoked organizational adaptation path, not evidence of unsolicited/background self-evolution. A user can also author templates manually; that parallel constructor path does not negate the autonomous mode. - External distinction: the skill begins from the user&#39;s/project&#39;s changed high-level operating requirement and existing team state rather than only internal chain-of-thought or backlog ordering. - Future / prospective distinction: the generated or modified templates define roles, workflow and capabilities for subsequent agent/team runs. - Adaptation option generated: add/modify a role, alter worker/orchestrator instructions, choose a sequential/parallel/debate/review workflow, change communication flow or package/reference a new skill. - Path back into current capability / S3: the generated templates are directly startable by Scion; the orchestrator then creates/manages workers under the changed role/workflow contract, making the adaptation operative in later current-control and S1 behavior.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure path is established for the Scion project/team at the chosen recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: system prompts, templates, RBAC, secret scopes, project settings and lifecycle authority constrain identity/access/operation, but no reviewed standard path turns a contested organizational-purpose or ultimate-policy issue into a legitimate final decision that is returned to govern the team. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S5 level. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: template/system-prompt text, project settings, profiles, access controls, ancestry scopes, user permissions and human-reserved deletion of leads/initiators. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Boundary reachability: no qualifying agent-owned or parent-governed identity/ultimate-policy loop was found in the standard distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: editable prompts/config and human authority over ordinary lifecycle actions do not become S5 without an identity/ultimate-policy issue, legitimate decision authority and a return path into subsequent operation. - Evidence: docs-site/src/content/docs/concepts.md; docs-site/src/content/docs/reference/agent-config.md; resources/platformskills/team-creation/SKILL.md; resources/platformskills/scion-agent-manage/references/agent-lifecycle.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the user remains the practical owner of many project-level choices, but generic user authority/configuration is not sufficient evidence for P. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: not established by the reviewed standard runtime. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: no S5 mode claimed. - Return-to-operation path: no identity/ultimate-policy closure path established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/46449a99c5ee41d135550b2068688dcf962bcc37/assessments/scion-gcp.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 46449a99c5ee41d135550b2068688dcf962bcc37\">46449a9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T18:07:25+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/theam/limina\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Limina</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/theam/limina/tree/56a4e2f3c9c613c7bc61f6a260ade379b21cd782\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 56a4e2f3c9c613c7bc61f6a260ade379b21cd782\">56a4e2f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform the research transformation by selecting hypotheses, experimental approaches, evidence collection, analysis and findings in the mission environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertain mechanisms, failed or conflicting experiments, variable baselines and unanticipated research obstacles. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select a concrete hypothesis/experiment, interpret results and decide the next research action. - Decision owner: autonomous Codex/Claude project research agent in managed mode, or the user-started agent in the installed template. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: H/E/F validation, canonical artifact IDs, workspace, engine continuation, database transactions, guardrails, mission limits and local templates. - Closure path: model-selected commands create/complete H/E/F and return evidence into the next research checkpoint/ACTIVE plan; the next model turn consumes durable state. - Boundary reachability: default managed runtime calls the first-party research service with a project-scoped capability; template AGENTS instructions and kb scripts operate from the generated project. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model decides substantive research hypotheses, procedure and interpretation; the runtime enforces transaction requirements but cannot select these research judgments with comparable discretion on its own. - Evidence: agent mission prompt and research tools, hypothesis and experiment creation, finding publication after evidence, template research loop. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the objective and limits originate with the parent; H/E/F file creation alone is not an autonomous judgment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: damp destructive concurrent experiment writes among separate research work cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two experiment lanes attempting to claim or mutate the same E artifact, stale versions overwriting newer evidence, or competing top-level strategic decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: grant or reject a per-experiment claim, isolate concurrent writes, and return lease conflicts to the agent lane for reallocation. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party database lease/CAS mechanism makes the immediate claim decision; a first-party specific coordination feedback path is supplied, but no separate autonomous S2-level conflict-resolution policy/agent is required or shown by the standard distribution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-experiment work leases, per-project coordinator lease, per-kind atomic ID counters, append-only observations, compare-and-swap decisions and scoped agent lane identity. - Closure path: rejected conflicting claims prevent the competing lane from writing; the caller receives a conflict and can select another E, wait or hand off. Successful separate experiment claims proceed concurrently. - Boundary reachability: managed runtime documents SDK subagent lanes and exposes the authenticated experiment.claim and experiment completion path to model/tool calls in the shipped server. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: lease adjudication is configured deterministic machinery, not an independently evidenced discretionary coordination agent; the provider may decide what to do after a conflict, but agent-owned S2 arbitration across lanes is not established as an operating requirement. - Distinct S1 units: independently operating experiment/subagent lanes, each conducting a bounded experiment and producing own observations. - Inter-S1 disturbance: duplicate claims and non-serializable edits to the same running experiment or stale findings. - Attenuating coordination relation: exclusive E-scoped leases, versioned completion and separate leases for distinct E artifacts. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a failed claim/invalid version is returned by first-party research commands; only the lease owner may complete its experiment, enabling retry/reallocation without clobbering observations. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the claim/lease is keyed to a demonstrated concrete concurrent evidence-writing conflict, not just to the order of tool calls or dispatch. - Evidence: architecture coordination matrix and subagent lanes, claim-experiment lease, completion owner check, lease conflict and CAS implementation. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a single-agent template project need not instantiate parallel S1 units; C is credited from the supported managed multi-lane constructor, not generic provider subagent capability.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate a research project&#39;s active commitments, objectives, running/waiting state and practical allocation of current attention under a coherent mission. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel experimental work diverging from the current priority, resource or provider failures, stalled decisions, unsatisfied access prerequisites, and operator reprioritization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive whole-project objective/step and whether research is RUNNING, WAITING, or COMPLETE; in parent mode, interrupt, reprioritize, pause or resume whole-project work. - Decision owner: base mode — project research model returns structured currentobjective/nextstep/status; parent mode — authenticated human OWNER/EDITOR reviews current state and supplies binding runtime lifecycle or high-priority guidance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: coordinator lease, versioned checkpoint, database/continuation store, retry backoff, runtime capability, human inbox and CLI/REST/MCP transport. - Closure path: agent checkpoint persists and becomes the next prompt/current research direction. Authenticated user intervention is sequenced into the durable inbox and delivered live or on wake; pause/stop interrupts and changes subsequent runtime execution. - Boundary reachability: all paths occur in the normal managed project supervisor and public project-operations layer, not dogfood-only governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic retries and hard lifecycle controls are support/parent authority; the model owns the discretionary research-priority checkpoint and cannot be replaced by the lease service without losing the same judgment. - Whole-system current view: project mission, ACTIVE snapshot, current coordinator/inbox, resource state, findings and project review status. - Current-control decision scope: present experiments, current objective, next step, blocker, active-turn interruption and commitment to completion/waiting. - Evidence: snapshot and model-owned structured checkpoint, provider-independent decision schema, authenticated steering/lifecycle operations, inbox return. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high for base; medium-high for separately owned parent mode. - Caveats: automatic failure retry alone would not qualify as autonomous S3.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: complementary, sufficiently independent access to operative evidence that can challenge routine experiment/reporting claims. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential self-reporting or measurement overstatement in a research finding. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party independent auditor with a separately governed corrective feedback path is established in the shipped managed or template mode. - Decision owner: not established; the producing research agent or ordinary human viewer can re-read/review evidence, but no independent audit ownership is wired. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: event telemetry, evidence revisions, review endpoints, validation scripts and optional devil&#39;s-advocate CR note templates. - Closure path: evidence review and challenge can change an agent&#39;s ordinary direction, but source does not require a materially independent complementary audit actor feeding the control loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the research-devil-advocate instruction asks the same model/session to criticise its current approach; this is reflective production judgment rather than an independent S3 path. The public review endpoint only exposes existing H/E/F state. - Evidence: review endpoint returns project status and records, devil&#39;s-advocate same-agent review skill, runtime feedback and telemetry. - Basis: structural bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: separately composed human/agent auditors could use exported raw data, but are not automatically shipped and closed as a first-party independent mode here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: investigate external research mechanisms and future options, challenge current strategic ceilings, and revise the project research capability/direction in dialogue with current work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing state of the art, unforeseen external constraints, invalid assumptions and future inadequacy of the present approach. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select an externally informed alternative mechanism/strategy and decide to continue, narrow, reset or pivot after comparing it with current evidence. - Decision owner: autonomous research agent in the installed template mode exercises the research-direction decision via external SOTA search and CR/SR; authenticated parent operator can separately steer future direction after viewing H/E/F evidence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: installable exploratory-sota-research and research-devil-advocate skills, L/CR/SR notes, mission/ACTIVE links, managed guidance and stored findings. - Closure path: external literature/challenge evidence generates prospective alternative H/SR; model commits revised direction into ACTIVE/next research objective and selects the next E. In parent mode, operator strategy decision enters durable guidance and is incorporated into the next model checkpoint, changing future work. - Boundary reachability: shipped template&#39;s AGENTS.md routes explicit research and strategic review through bundled skills when installed by first-party installskills workflow; managed first-party runtime also accepts outside inputs and persistent steering without borrowing repo-development dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model generates and judges candidate future directions, not just stores an external event or summarizes literature. A human can initiate a distinct supervisory future-direction decision through the supported review/steer path. - External distinction: landscape of mechanisms, external benchmark/reproducibility claims, risk and alternative approaches rather than only the current local task list. - Future / prospective distinction: whether a research strategy will generalize or plateau under future conditions, which mechanism family could replace it. - Adaptation option generated: CR/SR recommendation to pivot, reset, narrow or redesign experiments. - Path back into current capability / S3: revise kb/ACTIVE.md/currentobjective and subsequent H/E/F plan based on the external option and parent/agent decision. - Evidence: skill routing and strategic review flow, external SOTA candidate research to persisted artifacts, devil&#39;s-advocate pivot and ACTIVE update, strategic review direction template, human steer delivery. - Basis: explicit supported template mode, structural feedback. - Confidence: medium; scope is adaptation of the mission&#39;s research strategy, not proof that Limina autonomously rewrites its own product runtime. - Caveats: ordinary hypothesis selection alone is S1 rather than S4; this state is supported by the separate external/prospective research-and-pivot route. Some template skills require the documented installation step and are not silently assumed to be installed by every managed deployment.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: retain ultimate authority for the mission&#39;s identity, success criteria, risk boundaries and unresolved policy choices at parent recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a research outcome or new option conflicts with the human-defined mission, permitted resources, stopping conditions or evidence trust threshold. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether mission purpose, non-negotiable boundaries or blocked-stop authority should change; this is deliberately not delegated to autonomous research. - Decision owner: parent human creating and governing the project, not the Codex/Claude model and not the database validator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mission CHALLENGE.md, project success criteria, auth roles, escalation/steering inbox, user review, session recovery contract and runtime limits. - Closure path: first-party template instructs the agent to stop and ask for mission/strategic decisions; parent decides and changes authoritative challenge/boundaries or supplies durable guidance; on recovery/next prompt the agent rereads the challenge/accepted input and operates under it. In managed mode, original project identity is stored by user at kickoff and editable only in CREATED state; general steer is not mistaken for a guaranteed post-start database mission rewrite. - Boundary reachability: the template installation asks the operator for objective/boundaries/stopping conditions, runs in the generated project, and AGENTS.md instructs asking for mission decisions and reconstructing identity from CHALLENGE. This parent loop exists in documented first-party template mode without borrowing developer governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model may surface a policy choice but cannot legitimately redefine the user&#39;s research mission; system configuration and stored text enforce/transport a decision rather than owning ultimate authority. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: change permitted research scope, success criteria, ethical/resource boundary, or stop condition when discovery invalidates the original agreement. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: authenticated project owner / legitimate human sponsor in the template/managed project, no first-party autonomous S5 claimant. - Return-to-operation path: explicit question/blocker → parent decision → updated authoritative mission/guardrail or accepted guidance → model re-grounding and subsequent plan. - Evidence: first-party install skill collects mission and blocked stop, AGENTS mission authority, ask and recovery, agent prompt asks for mission decisions, project identity stored at creation, draft identity fields locked post-start. - Basis: explicit parent-governed project/template contract and structural closure. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: one-time mission setup or generic approval alone would not be enough; the credited parent mode includes escalation and returned identity-level decision. No automatic project-wide self-governance is claimed.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/be5e1da133ae8dc79a8121872500367da9295e69/assessments/li/limina.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit be5e1da133ae8dc79a8121872500367da9295e69\">be5e1da &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T20:47:56+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ntorga/agent-starter-kit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent Starter Kit</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ntorga/agent-starter-kit/tree/851e942ad94f98819cfe550da99c033444c10397\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 851e942ad94f98819cfe550da99c033444c10397\">851e942 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce scoped software implementation, design, context and review outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown code details, task failure, architecture facts and acceptance criteria. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose local implementation/investigation steps, execute native host tools, verify and return a handoff. - Decision owner: Registered model-backed framework personas, including Coder. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Supported host inference/tools, dispatch envelope, plan/todo/memory files and native/CLI transport. - Closure path: Maestro dispatches a persona identity plus bounded task → persona performs/validates work → structured handoff → correction or delivery. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The first-party persona specifies the autonomous action/feedback procedure; host machinery executes it. Coder chooses how to satisfy the brief while yielding scope/commit authority. - Evidence: README.md; ENTRYPOINT.md; personas/maestro.md; skills/dispatch/SKILL.md; personas/coder.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: high within the declared source boundary - Caveats: Static source evidence; no live model/provider run or empirical viability claim. - Boundary reachability: README installation and ENTRYPOINT boot wire the registered personas to explicit native and CLI dispatch instructions. No bespoke persona workflow is required after installation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate concrete shared-file interference among parallel coding personas. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent dispatches with overlapping file scopes can overwrite/conflict; too many concurrent jobs exceed the prescribed team concurrency. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Compare scopes, choose non-overlapping parallel groups and serialize unavoidable overlaps. - Decision owner: Maestro agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Dispatch envelopes, plan parallelizable-group annotations and host task/CLI execution. - Closure path: Scope declarations and plan/handoffs inform Maestro grouping; serialized or narrowed dispatch changes which persona can write which files next. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent chooses the grouping from actual scopes; the static limit and transport are supporting constraints, not ownership. - Evidence: skills/dispatch/SKILL.md; skills/plan-management/SKILL.md; personas/maestro.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: high within the declared source boundary - Caveats: Instruction-level scope regulation; no universal filesystem lock/compliance guarantee. - Boundary reachability: Maestro is required to load dispatch and plan-management; dispatch guardrails explicitly forbid overlap and cap simultaneous subagents at three. - Distinct S1 units: Two or more dispatched coders/personas performing independently scoped outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Overlapping parallel file writes and excessive concurrent dispatch load. - Attenuating coordination relation: Do not overlap file scopes; serialize when unavoidable; at most three parallel subagents. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Plans/handoffs identify current/next scopes; subsequent dispatches are regrouped, sequenced or narrowed. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The shipped rule directly addresses file-write collisions between units; dispatch is only its carrier.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate the installed team&#39;s current model resources, delivery commitments and exceptional intervention. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Low-confidence/low-quality provider output, task difficulty and new facts contradicting the agreed feature plan. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Base: choose/upgrade model tier, switch failed provider and redispatch/correct current work. Parent: decide changes to settled feature scope/commitments after escalation. - Decision owner: Base: Maestro. Parent: user who owns the project&#39;s agreed requirements and scope. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider/model registry, scoped dispatch, persistent plan/session state, Architect grounding and review gates. - Closure path: Base: handoff/review evidence → agent resource/provider/intervention choice → new dispatch. Parent: contradictory fact or changed scope → Maestro presents issue → user resolves → revised/re-grounded plan is reviewed → later Coder epic uses the returned decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model/resource and corrective commitment judgments are agent-owned in the base mode; settled-scope revision remains with the legitimate local user in the parent mode. Host CLI invocation and saved plans enforce/transport those choices. - Evidence: personas/maestro.md; skills/dispatch/SKILL.md; skills/plan-management/SKILL.md; skills/architect-impl-grounding/SKILL.md; skills/agent-memory/SKILL.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: high within the declared source boundary - Caveats: Parent control is at the local feature/team recursion; ordinary git commit approval alone is not its S3 witness. - Boundary reachability: Maestro dispatch escalation, dispatch tier/fallback rules and plan-management grounding/revision are mandatory parts of full orchestration; both modes are first-party. - Whole-system current view: Maestro receives persona handoffs, epic progress, review verdicts and current plan/session state across the installed team. - Current-control decision scope: Current provider/tier resources, replacement/corrective dispatch and user-reserved changes to the feature&#39;s settled commitments.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Complement reviewer truth/completeness reports with direct sampled operational evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Reviewers can hallucinate blockers or falsely report no findings while missing risky changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect raw code to confirm reported findings and sample omitted error/auth/data-mutation/I/O paths, then correct reviewer assumptions, provider or framework instructions. - Decision owner: The review-loop assigns this audit-like judgment to Maestro, but autonomous authority is not fully established because Maestro&#39;s broad red line forbids direct scanning/hands-on work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Reviewer handoffs, changed-file list and direct code access through the host; verdict/redispatch state. - Closure path: The supplied procedure confirms/discards claims, samples missing findings and feeds diagnosed context/design/model limitations into changed briefs/framework/provider and redispatch; authority conflict must be resolved to claim autonomous closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A specific complementary judgment/feedback path is supplied, but composition must reconcile the designated actor&#39;s conflicting authority before A can be credited. Ordinary per-epic/final QA is not itself the positive witness. - Evidence: skills/review-loop/SKILL.md; personas/maestro.md; personas/reviewer.md; skills/dispatch/SKILL.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium; function-specific constructor established, autonomous authority unresolved - Caveats: The positive is the separate checking of reviewers&#39; claims, not mandatory QA merely renamed as audit. No independent review was executed in this assessment. - Boundary reachability: Maestro explicitly invokes skills/review-loop/SKILL.md; step 5 exposes the function-specific raw evidence and returned control procedure in the shipped full mode. This is a concrete constructor, not arbitrary reviewer extensibility. - Claim being audited: Reviewer blocker validity and completeness, including suspiciously clean verdicts on complex changes. - Ordinary reporting path: Reviewer pass/partial-pass/fail handoff from routine code/plan reviews. - Complementary access path: Maestro spot-checks claims against actual code, then samples the first three high-risk paths for missing findings. - Independence boundary: Different role from Coder and reporting Reviewer; direct raw code can challenge their reports. Designated Maestro authority conflicts with its no-scanning red line, so fully closed autonomous independence/ownership is not asserted. - Who acts on findings: Maestro is specified to discard false positives and alter context/instructions/provider or redispatch; a composed authority resolution is still needed.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No external-and-prospective adaptation loop for this installed team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider changes, reviewer errors and accumulated observations may motivate improvement. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying first-party decisive right established for this function. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The cited runtime and documentation are supporting surfaces, not decision owners. - Closure path: No function-specific returned closure at this boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Switching a failing current provider, repairing ambiguous briefs, storing lessons or pulling an updated framework addresses present execution/internal history; no prospective external-option loop is established. - Evidence: skills/boot/SKILL.md; skills/agent-memory/SKILL.md; skills/review-loop/SKILL.md; skills/plan-management/SKILL.md; personas/maestro.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No identity/ultimate-policy issue and authoritative return loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User intent, safety/commit rules and plan contradictions limit local work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying first-party decisive right established for this function. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The cited runtime and documentation are supporting surfaces, not decision owners. - Closure path: No function-specific returned closure at this boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Plan scope, acceptance and provider choices are current control; static red lines, risk confirmations and memory preferences are not ultimate-identity closure. - Evidence: personas/maestro.md; skills/agent-decision/SKILL.md; skills/agent-memory/SKILL.md; skills/plan-management/SKILL.md; rules/README.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e72d8122bea991d98f228af63739c589468a14f0/assessments/ag/agent-starter-kit.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e72d8122bea991d98f228af63739c589468a14f0\">e72d812 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-18T16:02:42+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/majiayu000/harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rust Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-18\">2026-10-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/majiayu000/harness/tree/7f75dee2cb2eaf7195c9b8938ac76db691f1d157\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7f75dee2cb2eaf7195c9b8938ac76db691f1d157\">7f75dee &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce coding changes or analyses by running environment-coupled external-model-backed coding agents under the first-party harness lifecycle. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Novel issue requirements, source/test failures, changing repository files and live agent/tool return results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The configured coding model selects relevant next edits/tools and incorporates results; first-party adapter/session/process creates the executable environment and returns output/evidence. - Decision owner: Project-registered coding-agent runtime actor instantiated through supported Claude/Codex/OpenCode adapters. External provider supplies inference service; the agent task operates within Harness&#39; managed execution boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: harness exec, tool/permission scope, agent spawn supervisor, sandbox/proxy, workflow runtime job, attempt/lease record and result. - Closure path: User issue/instruction → agent adapter launch → model-chosen action/tool → repository/file observation → subsequent agent step → recorded task artifact/PR outcome. - Boundary reachability: Shipped CLI quickstart runs actual coding agent, and fleet dispatcher enqueues registered runtime activity to the source adapter; absent provider access would block execution rather than silently replace agent choice with a fake internal model. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Source code supplies boundaries and enforces permissions; the model-backed coding actor chooses context-dependent edits, unlike a deterministic prewritten macro. - Evidence: codexadapter.rs:235-330, spawnsupervisor.rs:1-135, dispatcher.rs:139-335, README quickstart. - Basis: Explicit model/agent/task return path in actual supported deployment. - Confidence: High source-structural, no hosted provider execution run for this assessment. - Caveats: Provider tool auth/sandbox are prerequisites; static policy denial does not independently perform coding work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate resource contention/priority crowding between separately running agent workflows sharing a constrained runtime-profile daily budget. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent claimable work under different runtime profiles can exhaust shared profile-specific budget bands or starve lower-usage profiles, creating unfair cross-agent dispatch competition. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A configurable policy chooses runtime profile limits and dailythrottleratio; first-party dispatcher senses usage and an alternative claimable profile and then defers the over-threshold profile&#39;s command in Enforce mode. - Decision owner: The operator owns threshold/prioritization policy; first-party dispatcher implements a function-specific composition contract, not a model-owned discretionary negotiation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: RuntimeProfileSelector, shared usage accounting, peekclaimablecommands, throttle-band detector, dispatch barrier/defer backoff and shadow/event auditing. - Closure path: Multiple independently executing agent workflows → competing runtime-profile budget spend and claimable commands → below-threshold alternative detected → overused profile command deferred with reason → other profile can obtain dispatch → affected agent tasks execute in revised order. - Boundary reachability: Source-owned RuntimeCommandDispatcher::dispatchcommand calls budgetgateoutcome; in Enforce mode calls deferclaimedcommandifowned, with a separate Shadow mode explicitly marked non-enforcing. This path is a shipped runtime fleet feature, not only a test. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The threshold and fairness criterion are human-configured, and the dispatcher executes that criterion. The first-party source supplies a real cross-workflow interference-to-return path, but not a credited autonomous owner of S2 deliberation; hence constructor C, not A. - Distinct S1 units: At least two concurrently claimable distinct coding-agent runtime jobs/workflows, potentially assigned different provider/runtime profiles. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Saturating one active profile in shared dispatch slots while alternative active agent work is below its budget/usage band. - Attenuating coordination relation: Configured dailythrottlebreach compares profile spend and selectable pending alternative then conditionally yields/defers a dispatch slot to avoid starvation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Enforce-mode deferclaimedcommandifowned applies a barrier/backoff, allowing an under-threshold task to proceed first. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: It uses measured inter-profile resource competition and reorders work based on other live claimable actors rather than merely following a hard-coded DAG or static dependency. - Evidence: dispatcherthrottle.rs:1-127, dispatcher.rs:375-500, dispatcher.rs:139-335. - Basis: Bounded constructible mutual coordination path with present enforcement wiring; owner config must supply policy. - Confidence: Medium for functional S2; distinct profile workload is supported but actual live contention not reproduced. - Caveats: If only one task/profile exists, nothing is mutually coordinated; Shadow does not enforce S2, and threshold policy does not originate autonomously.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No evidenced legitimate whole-current manager that makes binding tradeoffs across separate viable delivery units&#39; competing live commitments using a whole-system view. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Fleet cost/capacity pressure, PR/review backlog, stalled tasks and runtime-host outages, mostly handled by configured local barriers and per-workflow support. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator-configured budget ceilings and per-workflow cancel/unblock/retry are supported; no concrete cross-unit commitment/priority decision source uses a whole-current view and closes such a choice. - Decision owner: Operator for global threshold configuration and local intervention, deterministic workflow/dispatch machinery for applying them; not a first-party autonomous S3 governor or proven parent-owned S3-specific transaction at this recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Operator monitor, aggregate workflow health/status, registered runtime hosts, configured daily caps, queue budgets, status/repair control APIs. - Closure path: Workflow issue → per-workflow action; missing established fleet-level shared-commitment judgement based on whole-current status → allocated/renegotiated division resources → observed changed collective operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Observability and strong command execution alone do not demonstrate who decides system-wide resource bargains. The adaptive dispatch path addresses S2 contention under fixed limits, not S3 strategy for all commitments. - Whole-system current view: Runtime monitor aggregates active workflow states, action eligibilities, capacity and host health, but that is a support view, not itself a decision right. - Current-control decision scope: Configured workload budget and individual workflow cancellation/retry; insufficient evidence of actual whole-fleet priority/commitment reallocation tied to that view. - Evidence: mod.rs:167-275, mod.rs:486-575, dispatcher.rs:375-500, API contract. - Basis: Source-bounded absence of whole-current decision/closure under the examined modes. - Confidence: Medium; the operator UI is a credible future parent S3 surface but cannot be graded positive solely for existing. - Caveats: A human operator in an independently managed organization may use this cockpit for S3, but their unobserved off-platform governance cannot be credited here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Obtain independent current reality about model-agent-delivered coding work beyond the implementing agent&#39;s own completion summary and force corrective action before integration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Erroneous claims of completed implementation, stale/dirty PR head, skipped requirements, faulty code, unresolved review threads, unvalidated changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Separately invoked model-backed local reviewer inspects current repository head and original requirements, independently decides pass/changes/blocked and returns findings; first-party reducer moves the workflow accordingly. - Decision owner: Model-backed review agent in the separate local-review activity with independent review context; deterministic validation of signal/head/CI and GitHub snapshot is enforcement rather than the substantive model review. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Review activity, current-head SHA/clean-tree evidence, separate read-only review prompt, structured findings/signals, review-state reducer, queued repair task and exact-head readiness requirements. - Closure path: Implementer claims PR ready → independent local-review activity obtains current source/head and initial issue plan → LocalReviewChangesRequested or Blocked → workflow addressingfeedback with new remediation agent activity, or blocked → repaired current PR → new review/merge gate. - Boundary reachability: The shipped githubissuepr flow enqueues runlocalreview at a dedicated state, consumes LocalReviewOutcome and triggers addressprfeedback on findings, while a separate direct local reviewer runtime is supported. Not borrowed only from an external CI job. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model reviewer examines raw current PR independently and originates substantive findings; source-only signal parsing enforces the resulting audit decision and returns it. - Claim being audited: Agent-written completion/current PR readiness against original requirements and verified worktree at a specific commit. - Ordinary reporting path: Implementing coding agent&#39;s task/artifact/PR repair summary and claimed completion/validation. - Complementary access path: A separate fresh-context model-backed reviewer receives actual PR head/issue plan, checks working tree and evidence, and can refuse to accept the author&#39;s unsupported assertion. - Independence boundary: Separate activity/reviewer task from agent implementation and repair; independent review instructions explicitly require treating prior repairs as claims. The same provider family could be configured in two jobs, so independence is procedural, not guaranteed vendor separation. - Who acts on findings: The first-party workflow reducer queues further addressprfeedback, blocks or withholds readiness and can repeat local review before merge. - Evidence: prfeedback.rs:301-428, mod.rs:580-605, tick.rs:105-220. - Basis: Separate inquiry and authoritative blocking/correction return into current coding work, beyond routine implementer self-report. - Confidence: Medium-high source-structural; actual live PR review not run in this assessment. - Caveats: Ordinary tests/CI alone would not establish S3. Optional periodic review may corroborate but is not required for this independent local-review closed path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Observe repeated quality/environment failures, generate future-oriented new rule/skill or guard options, then allow valid learned policy to change subsequent harness operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing repository/project error patterns, repeated rule violations, chronic blockers, slow sessions and code hotspots exceed static prior protections. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A configured GC coding agent examines gathered signals and chooses future remediation artifact content; bounded optional rules-only auto-adoption writes accepted artifacts, then scheduled first-party learning constructs rules into the current rules engine. - Decision owner: Model-backed GC adaptation agent for content selection; operator&#39;s standing rules-only path/prefix policy is a limit, not the individual remediation choice. First-party live learnrules model call can further select executable rule candidates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: EventStore and scan checkpoint, signal detector and priority ranking, GC AgentRequest tool/budget limits, draft store, status/Pending/Adopted transitions, rule-only path gates, periodic selfevolution and rules-engine insertion. - Closure path: Prior recurring warnings/violations from real operation → sampled/aggregated signal → model-backed GC draft proposal → autoadoptmatching in opt-in RulesOnly mode and project-path validation → adopted draft → periodic learnrules agent produces parsed rules → rules engine addrule → new operational checks in later coding workflows. - Boundary reachability: QualityTrigger grades post-task observations and calls GcAgent.run; its optional autoadopt policy calls real filesystem adoption. The bundled server selfevolution::start schedules learnrules; handler reads adopted drafts and installs rule definitions into live engine. This is a shipped executable opt-in route, not a roadmap. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Hard-coded thresholds and parsers alone would not generate new context-sensitive remediations from emergent external errors. Source invokes a coding agent with observed signals, and separately a learning agent to turn adopted evidence into rules. - External distinction: Observed project execution quality, repository error patterns and recurring agent/tool failures outside the previous rule coverage. - Future / prospective distinction: Drafted guards/rules/skills are for later workflows, not merely retrying the same failed tool action. Adopted rule content changes subsequently enforced capability. - Adaptation option generated: New rule/guard/skill remediation derived from prior empirical warnings, with rules-only automatic adoption supported; defaults remain conservative and do not auto-adopt. - Path back into current capability / S3: Generated GC rule draft → bounded adoption → agent-extracted rule → state.engines.rules.write().await.addrule → later code-validation/rule-check operations (S1). There is no fabricated autonomous S3 just to close S4. - Evidence: gcagent.rs:106-222, gcagent.rs:260-363, qualitytrigger.rs:320-415, learn.rs:13-84, selfevolution.rs:25-103, misc.rs:180-235. - Basis: Model-selected prospective remedies reach a future runtime rule engine through shipped opt-in closure. - Confidence: Medium source-structural due to opt-in config and dependency on actual newly generated artifact validity. - Caveats: Default Off leaves pending drafts; no source claims the model always emits path-qualified eligible rule artifacts; runtime hosted inference and efficacy improvements not replicated.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No evidenced ultimate identity/purpose constitutional arbitration with legitimate authority and actual return into global organizational policy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Credential/network scope, project identity, task permissions, workflow configuration and merge authorization control execution safety, not the fundamental purpose/identity of the delivery organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Project operators configure credentials and standing rules and may approve a merge, but no in-boundary S5 actor resolves a purpose/identity conflict at the stated recursion. - Decision owner: External platform adopters set organizational mission; shipped runtime&#39;s rule engine, authentication and isolated tool permissions enforce delegated limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: HTTP auth, agent capability contracts, workflow definitions, Starlark rules and execution sandbox. - Closure path: Operator config → policy enforcement; no detected identity crisis/proposal → ultimate legitimate authority → returned revised purpose guiding future cross-system operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A gate that blocks access or asks for approval cannot turn into final constitutional authority merely because it is cryptographically or procedurally enforced. - Evidence: workflow.rs:72-95, misc.rs:180-235, API contract, dispatcher.rs:139-335. - Basis: First-party supported boundary absence, not an impossibility for a larger real organization. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Adopter corporate governance and contributor authorization are outside the first-party source boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c6e8a2f5d0677ca200001fe7f5bb01c85d029c61/assessments/rust-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c6e8a2f5d0677ca200001fe7f5bb01c85d029c61\">c6e8a2f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-04T15:35:15+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yuanchuan/aivo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Aivo Code</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-04\">2026-10-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yuanchuan/aivo/tree/b26438571e80ae28fa489f07c2c22fd595e10269\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b26438571e80ae28fa489f07c2c22fd595e10269\">b264385 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform environment-facing software-engineering work by interpreting a task, selecting coding/tools, executing them, observing results and iterating to a completed or typed terminal outcome. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous codebases and requests, changing files/Git/process state, compiler/test/tool failures, permissions, provider responses, context pressure, interruptions, background work and implementation choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose what to inspect, which tool or subagent to invoke, what edit/command to perform next, how to react to returned evidence and when to finish or continue. - Decision owner: the main model-backed Aivo Code agent instantiated by AgentEngine. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: first-party turn loop; file/process/search tools; plan/finish tools; MCP; skills; permissions; retries/guards; session persistence; context compaction; TUI/headless hosts. - Closure path: user task + current workspace/session → model selects action/tool → Aivo executes or gates it → result enters conversation → next model request sees the updated state → next action or terminal outcome. - Boundary reachability: aivo code and aivo code -e instantiate the same first-party engine directly. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-backed actor removes the open-ended coding judgment; deterministic runtime code remains transport/enforcement only. - Evidence: src/agent/engine/turn.rs; src/commands/engineassembly.rs; src/commands/codeagentoneshot.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model inference comes from an external provider; Aivo is credited for the first-party decision/tool/feedback organization around it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate concrete interference among concurrently executing delegated coding S1s by separating their mutable repository state when the main agent judges isolation necessary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel file-mutating delegates can write the same shared workspace and collide, overwrite, or observe each other&#39;s partial changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide, in the model-authored subagent call, to request isolation: &quot;worktree&quot; for an editing delegate, especially when several delegates are fanned out in parallel. - Decision owner: the main model-backed Aivo Code agent. A profile may also predeclare isolation, but the standard tool contract independently exposes the same coordination decision to the agent at runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: concurrent subagent worker pool; model-visible isolation argument; disposable Git worktrees; per-delegate cwd; worktree guards/finalization; fail-closed refusal when requested isolation cannot be created for a parallel delegate. - Closure path: main agent identifies/fans out parallel delegated work → chooses worktree isolation for writers → Aivo creates separate worktrees before concurrent execution → delegates operate without shared-write collision → each result/worktree disposition returns to the parent → parent integrates or continues. - Boundary reachability: normal Aivo Code engine assembly advertises the subagent tool and its model-callable isolation: &quot;worktree&quot; argument; several leading subagent calls in one ordinary model tool batch enter the built-in parallel worker pool, so this is a shipped runtime path rather than test-only plumbing. - Distinct S1 units: two or more fresh delegated Aivo AgentEngine sub-engines running concurrently from one parent tool-call batch, each executing a bounded model/tool coding task. - Inter-S1 disturbance: parallel file-mutating delegates in the same checkout can collide through shared writes, overwrite or observe one another&#39;s partial changes, and make the combined workspace result unreliable. - Attenuating coordination relation: the parent model selects worktree isolation for the affected delegates; Aivo binds each isolated child to a disposable Git worktree and, for a parallel delegate that requested isolation, refuses to fall back to the shared workspace if isolation cannot be created. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: isolation changes each child S1&#39;s actual workspace before it acts; after the parallel batch, each delegated result and worktree disposition returns as parent tool results so the main S1 can decide subsequent integration, review, or follow-up. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the positive mapping rests on an explicit inter-S1 concurrent-write collision and a first-party worktree relation chosen to attenuate that disturbance. Parallel delegation, session mail, worker-pool scheduling and result fan-in are not credited by themselves. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime deterministically enforces the requested boundary, but the substantive choice that a delegate should be isolated is available to and selected by the main agent from the tool schema. Without that model decision, the generic path can run shared-workspace delegates. - Evidence: src/agent/engine/toolbatch.rs; src/agent/engine/subagent.rs; src/agent/builtinskills/create-agent.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Aivo does not force every parallel delegate into a worktree. The credited autonomous S2 mode is the supported runtime path where the agent explicitly chooses isolation to attenuate the documented writer-collision disturbance.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct first-party whole-organization current-control function was established beyond the focal main coding agent&#39;s own task execution and bounded delegation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: plans, budgets, permission state, context/step ceilings, subagent progress, background jobs and session messages are controlled operationally, but no separate whole-system current portfolio/resource/accountability control role is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S3 level. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: updateplan; finishturn; typed stops; subagent status; background jobs; permissions; TUI /goal; session mail; runtime guards. - Closure path: not applicable; no distinct whole-current state aggregation → metasystem intervention → changed multi-S1 operational allocation/accountability loop was reconstructed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main agent can delegate and observe bounded work while performing its own S1 task, but that does not by itself create a separate S3 function. - Evidence: src/agent/engine/turn.rs; src/agent/engine/toolbatch.rs; src/agent/engine/sessioncomms.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this negative state does not deny substantial lifecycle control inside a coding turn.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge a producing coding agent&#39;s change/completion using a separate model-backed reviewer with complementary read-only access and evidence-backed verdicts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the producing agent may believe its implementation is correct or complete while the actual diff, tests, commands or edge cases reveal failure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the live workspace/change through a separate review context, decide PASS/FAIL or APPROVE/REQUEST CHANGES from observed evidence, and return that verdict/report to the parent agent. - Decision owner: the separate model-backed Aivo subagent instantiated from the compiled-in verification or evaluate profile. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in review profiles compiled into the binary; fresh delegated AgentEngine; no edit/write tools; read/grep/glob/list/run tools; real command/test execution; bounded subagent run; parent subagent result path; optional deterministic post-edit self-verification as corroborating evidence. - Closure path: producing main agent has a change/completion claim → invokes built-in verification/evaluate subagent → separate reviewer reads current workspace/diff and runs independent checks → reviewer emits an evidence-backed verdict → Aivo returns it as the parent tool result → main agent sees the verdict and can revise or reconfirm subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: the review profiles are compiled-in defaults discovered by normal Aivo Code engine assembly; no downstream custom reviewer implementation is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic Aivo code constructs the sub-engine and tool boundary, but the substantive audit findings/verdict are generated by a separate model-backed reviewer. Removing that reviewer removes the semantic audit judgment. - Evidence: src/agent/subagents.rs; src/agent/builtinagents/verification.md; src/agent/builtinagents/evaluate.md; src/agent/engine/subagent.rs; src/commands/engineassembly.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: review invocation is optional and model-selected; the state records a supported first-party audit mode, not a claim that every Aivo Code turn is independently reviewed. - Claim being audited: the producing main agent&#39;s implementation/change and its implied correctness/completion claim. - Ordinary reporting path: the producing agent&#39;s normal tool loop, text answer and finishturn completion path. - Complementary access path: a distinct sub-engine with no editing tools reads the live change/workspace and can run real tests/commands; edit-less review intentionally stays on the shared workspace so uncommitted producer changes remain visible. - Independence boundary: separate model invocation/session state, separate specialist system instructions and tool scope, no nested delegation, and no write/edit capability for the built-in verification/evaluate profiles. - Who acts on findings: the main Aivo Code agent receives the subagent&#39;s report as a tool result and controls subsequent coding/completion behavior.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material autonomous outside-and-future intelligence loop that selects and persists an organizational adaptation was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: skills, agent profiles, session history, model/provider choice and project guides can change available capability/context, but the standard runtime does not autonomously scan future environmental change and adopt a persistent organizational redesign on that basis. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: user/project configuration or ordinary task-level model action, depending on the surface. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent agent profiles; create-agent skill; skills; project guides; session state; MCP; model/provider configuration. - Closure path: not applicable; no environmental/future distinction → adaptation-option generation → autonomous selection → persistent capability/strategy change → later operation closure was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model can author a profile when asked and Aivo can rediscover it mid-run, but generic authoring capability is not by itself a prospective S4 loop. - Evidence: src/agent/subagents.rs; src/agent/builtinskills/create-agent.md; src/commands/engineassembly.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream workflow may use Aivo tools to implement adaptation; that organization is outside this standalone assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permissions, auto-approval, sandbox boundaries, project guides, model/provider settings, agent profiles and hooks constrain behavior, but they are operating policy/configuration rather than a reconstructed identity-level governance function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an identity/ultimate-policy issue. - Decision owner: user/operator/project configuration for the relevant policies; Aivo runtime enforces them and agents operate within them. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission ladder; grants; sandbox escalation; hooks; project guides; model/provider configuration; agent profiles; approval UI/headless fail-closed behavior. - Closure path: not applicable at S5 level; no genuine identity/policy conflict → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative identity decision → returned operation loop was established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither the main coding model nor review subagents own Aivo Code&#39;s ultimate identity/policy boundary. - Evidence: src/agent/permission.rs; src/commands/engineassembly.rs; src/commands/codeagentoneshot.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: human/operator policy can govern a broader organization that uses Aivo; that parent is not imported without a function-specific parent-mode closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b5364bd04c506e6a4f267e549f8731cf016a0a50/assessments/aivo-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b5364bd04c506e6a4f267e549f8731cf016a0a50\">b5364bd &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T23:44:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cft0808/edict\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Edict</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-24\">2026-10-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cft0808/edict/tree/14a207557719c046af0f993a7bff1cc5a5015b33\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 14a207557719c046af0f993a7bff1cc5a5015b33\">14a2075 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute a scoped ministry task (engineering, docs, analysis, testing or infrastructure) and return an observed outcome. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool and code errors, variable repository state, incomplete outputs and other task-specific uncertainties. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A supported installed model-backed ministry agent chooses task actions/tools and adjusts behavior after observations and results. - Decision owner: The model-backed installed OpenClaw ministry agent; Edict supplies its bundled role and task-state interface, not the external model runtime&#39;s entire governance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Installed role SOUL.md, OpenClaw agent workspace, subagent permission matrix, file-backed task/log commands and progress reporting. - Closure path: Assigned task → ministry agent tool work and observation → further action or completion → returned result and Edict task/progress state. - Boundary reachability: install.sh packages role prompts and configures the existing OpenClaw agents; ministry roles and delegation are an explicit supported full-install workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Choices of implementation, testing and task-specific actions are model-directed in the composed installed mode; deterministic state files and dashboard do not themselves make choices. - Evidence: installer roles, work allocation and return contract, one ministry role. - Basis: Shipped provider-composed agent feedback/action mode. - Confidence: Medium-high for install path and role capability; no live task execution measured. - Caveats: Running the demo-only Docker dashboard does not yield this state.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No distinct closed first-party cross-ministry interference attenuation path is established beyond role assignment and fixed task routing at the reviewed installed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple ministry S1 workers could duplicate edits, compete for tools or produce incompatible changes, but a clear inter-S1 conflict detector and feedback resolution mechanism is not shown in the credited mode. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Not established as S2-specific in the first-party installed path. The model-backed Shangshu assigns what to each ministry at S3, and the gate reviews plans; ordinary messages and permissions do not show a conflict-driven coordinator relation. - Decision owner: No separate S2 owner evidenced. Experimental Redis dispatch semaphores are mechanical agent/task capacity and duplicate-delivery control, not autonomous inter-S1 conflict judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Department jurisdiction table, subagent allowAgents matrix, task/flow logs, status board and optional event-worker per-agent semaphore. - Closure path: Normal delegation and collection return to Shangshu, but no demonstrated cross-S1 interference signal → resolution → changed sibling operations is independently closed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A division of ministries, shared data and an execution queue are not sufficient under Methodology 0.3.7 without the specific inter-S1 disturbance witness. - Evidence: ministry routing, fixed OpenClaw permissions, separate backend dispatch limit. - Basis: Bounded negative: role routing is not independently evidenced S2 conflict attenuation. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: User-specific OpenClaw subagent extensions might implement stronger conflict management; those are not attributed to Edict.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Govern current commitments of one edict by selecting ministry assignments, monitoring their progress and deciding how to integrate or re-dispatch current work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Heterogeneous workload, partially finished ministry outputs, misassigned tasks, missing results and current delivery failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The installed model-backed Shangshu dispatch role decides which ministries undertake subtasks and how to respond to their results; the planning role can revise the current plan after gate rejection. - Decision owner: The composed OpenClaw Shangshu/Zhongshu control actors, distinct from the fixed transition script and operator dashboard. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan/todo state, assigned ministries, shared task files, progress reports, allowAgents, dashboard status and OpenClaw subagent call return. - Closure path: Whole-edict task/plan view → selected dispatch across specialized S1 cells → progress/results from ministries → Shangshu synthesizes and returns to Zhongshu/user or requests further work. - Boundary reachability: The first-party installer wires all referenced roles and their allowable delegations; bundled Shangshu instructions explicitly require progress inspection, assignment and aggregate response. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Selection of the department portfolio for a particular edict is agent discretion; predetermined state ordering and dispatch event handling are only carriers. - Whole-system current view: The edict&#39;s agreed plan, department task assignments, cross-ministry progress updates and result summaries under one edict ID; not every independent deployment or organization-wide roadmap. - Current-control decision scope: Departmental resource commitments, who acts now, synthesis and return of ongoing work, plus bounded recovery/replanning of the same edict. - Evidence: Shangshu current-control instructions, role composition, task-state map. - Basis: Shipped control-role discretion returned into current multi-ministry activity. - Confidence: Medium; no actual model-driven portfolio decision was tested. - Caveats: Scope is one edict, not project-level whole-portfolio S3. Manual stop/cancel/resume does not separately justify (P).\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Audit proposed operational plan claims independently of the planning actor before releasing the task to execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unworkable steps, missing subtasks, underestimated risks and inaccurate planning assumptions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The separately configured Menxia review agent decides to approve (准奏) or veto (封驳) and return concrete corrective objections; rejection routes back to Zhongshu. - Decision owner: Model-backed Menxia agent in a separate installed OpenClaw role/workspace, not the Zhongshu planner whose claims it audits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Review rubric, command to return task state to Zhongshu or advance to Assigned, flow logs, allowAgents and finite retry guidance. - Closure path: Zhongshu plan → Menxia&#39;s separate feasibility/completeness/risk/resource inspection → veto/approval → re-planning or authorized execution. - Boundary reachability: Menxia workspace and role are installed by the standard script and permitted in the Zhongshu delegation chain; its SOUL.md gives executable board/return commands. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Menxia owns the plan-quality verdict, while transition scripts only record/enforce the chosen result. Reviewer independence is role/workspace and contrary authority, not proven unrelated model weights or institution. - Claim being audited: The planning department&#39;s proposed decomposition is sound and executable. - Ordinary reporting path: Zhongshu writes planning steps/todos and presents its proposed scheme. - Complementary access path: Separate Menxia role reviews the proposal under feasibility, completeness, risk and resource criteria and writes its own review result. - Independence boundary: Different installed agent identity, workspace, prompt and assigned role, with explicit veto even against the planner. It remains inside the same operator/provider ecosystem. - Who acts on findings: Zhongshu revises after a veto; approvals permit Shangshu to allocate work. - Evidence: Menxia audit criteria and executable veto, first-party role installation and permissions, process architecture. - Basis: A distinct review actor judges another controller&#39;s operational claim with direct return to the current control cycle. - Confidence: Medium; prompt-described veto is not a measured runtime guarantee. - Caveats: Menxia instructions force approval on the third review round; that seriously bounds the integrity of the gate and must not be described as an unconditional permanent veto. No live review efficacy study.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No evidenced first-party loop that turns sensed external/future environmental changes into adopted future delivery-system capability decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Emerging models, news, tooling and external requirements; the separately bundled news official can collect world/AI headlines, but news delivery is not yet an adaptation decision for Edict&#39;s task organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No S4-specific actor with option/adoption authority evidenced. User-triggered model changes and routine retrospective task choices remain separate paths. - Decision owner: Humans manually choose model assignments and skills; the brief agent selects news items, not Edict&#39;s future operating strategy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: News briefing official, headlines script, dashboard model selection, skill management and change application/restart script. - Closure path: News → digest for emperor does not show prospectively developed organizational options → authoritative adoption → changed future capability. Directly applying a user&#39;s selected model is configuration, not external intelligence. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Even a model-produced daily external digest need not constitute S4 unless its distinctions influence a future-oriented capability/strategy decision. - Evidence: news brief role, operator model-change script. - Basis: Bounded negative after inspecting plausible external-sensing and future-change surfaces. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Another external workflow could consume the daily brief and choose organizational adaptations, but none is shown as an operating first-party Edict mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party identity/ultimate-policy dispute handling and authoritative returned decision is established at the user-edict organization recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Disagreement over organizational mandate, legitimacy or immutable operating principles might arise, but installed role permissions handle delegation and task scope instead. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No actual ultimate-policy right exercised through a first-party agent or human parent escalation workflow is shown. The Emperor/user issues objectives and can intervene operationally, not evidenced identity-policy settlements. - Decision owner: Outside operator sets initial requirements, role allowlists and model preferences; no first-party S5-specific governing channel. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Role permission matrix, separation-of-powers prompts, dashboard stop/cancel/resume, task final approval and local OpenClaw configuration. - Closure path: These mechanisms change one task or allowlist, but no identity-level conflict → legitimate authority → ultimate decision → standing subsequent policy loop is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Invoking imperial titles, vetting one plan or applying an operational safety rule does not establish governance of organizational identity. - Evidence: installer permissions, README intervention feature, task transition model. - Basis: Bounded absence of identity/ultimate-policy closure, including parent path. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: An external human institution operating Edict may legitimately maintain S5, but the integration is not shown in these supported role/board paths.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f84d2a046b54a5ddd9fac72637281dcd530a5afe/assessments/ed/edict.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f84d2a046b54a5ddd9fac72637281dcd530a5afe\">f84d2a0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T13:34:39+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bigduu/Bamboo-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bamboo</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-26\">2026-09-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bigduu/Bamboo-agent/tree/eb1151378d513e820f80513c387056fb14f5c664\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision eb1151378d513e820f80513c387056fb14f5c664\">eb11513 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform user-directed work through a persistent model/tool feedback loop and alter the local/external task environment through first-party tools. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user tasks, workspace/process state, tool results and failures, model/provider responses, context limits, permissions, memory/skill context and follow-up observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive tool/action and arguments, interpret returned evidence, and decide whether to continue, delegate or conclude. - Decision owner: the active root Bamboo model actor; a child model separately owns operational choices inside its delegated assignment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runtime loop, tool registry/executor, durable session state, permissions/approvals, context compression, memory, skill/MCP surfaces, execution/recovery machinery and child runtime. - Closure path: user/task/session context → model chooses substantive tool/action → runtime executes/refuses and records result → later model round observes the result → model revises action or returns a final result. - Boundary reachability: the ordinary Bamboo server/runtime ships this model/tool execution path directly; no downstream component is required to obtain the operational loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime components execute, persist and constrain actions, but removing the model removes task-specific action selection and interpretation of operational feedback. - Evidence: README.md; crates/app/bamboo-server-tools/src/subagent.rs; ordinary runtime/engine architecture at the pinned ref. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate interference and redundant/overlapping work among concurrently executing child S1 units by assigning explicit non-overlapping responsibilities and mutation scopes, then steering or correcting children as live outcomes reveal conflicts or stalls. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel children may default to the same parent workspace and can otherwise duplicate work, pursue overlapping responsibilities, modify overlapping state, or continue on a stale/wrong assignment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide each child&#39;s responsibility, detailed task, workspace and relevant mutation boundary, and revise/steer/cancel that assignment when child status or outcomes show a coordination problem. - Decision owner: the root Bamboo model invoking the model-facing SubAgent tool. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: required responsibility field; schema guidance requiring narrow non-overlapping responsibility; child assignment frame making scope authoritative and restricting mutation to assigned scope; optional separate child workspace; background parallel execution; status guidance; sendmessage, update, cancel, wait and completion feedback. - Closure path: root model partitions work into explicit child responsibilities/workspaces → child assignment makes that scope authoritative during operation → concurrent children execute → current status/completion/error returns to root → root can redirect/retry/cancel/change scope → later child/root operation follows the revised coordination decision. - Boundary reachability: SubAgent is a standard first-party model-facing root tool and its child assignment/status machinery is wired through the shipped server/engine runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Bamboo supplies deterministic lifecycle and persistence, but the task-specific responsibility/workspace split and live redirection decisions are chosen by the root model. Removing the model leaves no equivalent semantic partition of the current job. - Evidence: crates/app/bamboo-server-tools/src/subagent.rs; crates/engine/bamboo-engine/src/sessionapp/childsession/helpers.rs; crates/engine/bamboo-engine/src/sessionapp/childcompletioncoordinator.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Bamboo does not establish a hard per-file disjoint-write lease at this ref; the credited attenuation is the model-owned non-overlapping responsibility/mutation-scope contract plus live steering, so badly chosen scopes can still collide. - Distinct S1 units: separately persisted root/child model-tool sessions; multiple children may be created in one turn and run concurrently in background. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent children may share the parent workspace and could duplicate or overlap work/state mutation without explicit task boundaries. - Attenuating coordination relation: required narrow, non-overlapping model-selected responsibilities plus authoritative per-child scope/mutation instructions and optional workspace separation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: child status/results/errors return to the root; status guidance and sendmessage/update/cancel/rerun paths let the root alter the affected child&#39;s subsequent execution rather than blindly spawning redundant work. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited relation is not child spawning or mailbox availability alone; it is explicitly aimed at separating concurrent responsibilities/mutations that can interfere in a shared workspace and closing observed problems through changed child behavior.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: supervise the current root/child organization as a whole, allocate active child commitments/resources and intervene in live child work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing child portfolio, running/pending/error/completed state, stalls, wrong assignments, model/cost tradeoffs, need for early error response and changing current task commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: create or withhold child commitments; select responsibility, workspace, model, reasoning effort and lifecycle; inspect current children; update/rerun/steer/cancel/delete them; and choose whole-set wait policy before integrating results. - Decision owner: the root Bamboo model actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SubAgent.list/get/create/update/run/sendmessage/cancel/delete/wait; child index/session persistence; runtime status guidance; wait policy; completion coordinator and automatic parent resume. - Closure path: root creates current child portfolio → children execute and expose current state/results → root lists/gets or waits on the portfolio → root changes assignments/resources/steering or terminates work as needed → runtime applies the chosen intervention and subsequent operation proceeds under the new commitments. - Boundary reachability: these controls are available directly to the root model through the ordinary shipped SubAgent tool; they do not depend on test/dev orchestration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: wait scheduling, persistence and cancellation mechanics enforce decisions, but the root model owns the discretionary allocation and intervention choices over the current child organization. - Evidence: crates/app/bamboo-server-tools/src/subagent.rs; crates/engine/bamboo-engine/src/sessionapp/childsession/helpers.rs; crates/engine/bamboo-engine/src/sessionapp/childcompletioncoordinator.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: a simple single-agent run does not exercise S3; the state is established in the shipped multi-child mode. No distinct parent-governed S3 mode was established from the inspected operator/UI surfaces. - Whole-system current view: list exposes the active child roster and get/completion/status machinery exposes each child&#39;s current or terminal state; wait can reason over every active child or an explicit subset under a root-selected policy. - Current-control decision scope: current child commitments, responsibilities, workspaces, model/reasoning allocation, lifecycle, steering, retries, cancellation/deletion and wait/integration timing.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: independently challenge the root agent&#39;s terminal completion claim using a separate read-only adversarial reviewer that inspects workspace evidence and returns an approve/reject verdict with findings. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the executing agent can claim completion despite missed requirements, incorrect changed files, inadequate diff/test evidence, regressions or unverified criteria. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently inspect changed files and recorded verification evidence, decide whether stopping is justified, and reject with concrete findings that must be addressed before completion. - Decision owner: a separate Guardian reviewer model in the implemented constructor path; the ordinary server exposes no activation owner because it passes guardianconfig: None at this ref. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only Guardian child tool denylist, dedicated adversarial rubric, durable Guardian state, bounded review budget, verdict parser, synthetic reject on malformed/error output, completion coordinator and hidden parent-resume message. - Closure path: when the constructor is enabled, terminal point → runtime spawns read-only Guardian → Guardian independently reads/searches changed-file and recorded diff/test evidence → verdict is recorded → rejection resumes parent with findings and directs repair → a later terminal point can be reviewed again. The standard server stops before the trigger because Guardian configuration is not surfaced. - Boundary reachability: the complete S3-specific machinery and server-provided spawner are first-party and shipped, but crates/app/bamboo-server/src/handlers/agent/execute/runtime/execution.rs explicitly supplies guardianconfig: None with a TODO to expose configuration. A downstream/first-party composition step is therefore still required to activate the audit loop, so the publication state is Constructor rather than Autonomous. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the independent reviewer would own the audit judgment once activated; deterministic parsing/resume only enforces its verdict. No standard server run can currently instantiate that ownership mode from the supported request surface. - Evidence: crates/engine/bamboo-engine/src/runtime/guardianstate.rs; crates/engine/bamboo-engine/src/sessionapp/childcompletioncoordinator.rs; crates/engine/bamboo-engine/src/runtime/config.rs; crates/app/bamboo-server/src/handlers/agent/execute/runtime/execution.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: this is intentionally C, not A: engine completeness is not enough to borrow an activation path absent from the assessed server mode. - Claim being audited: the main/root agent&#39;s claim that the current task is complete and the run may terminate. - Ordinary reporting path: main agent reaches terminal completion using its own execution context and recorded work/test evidence. - Complementary access path: a separate read-only Guardian child with dedicated Read/Glob/Grep/GetFileInfo-style inspection access evaluates the actual changed files plus recorded diff/test evidence rather than trusting the main claim. - Independence boundary: distinct child session/model context, read-only tool surface, explicit adversarial rubric and separate verdict state; mutation/spawn/shell/web tools are removed. - Who acts on findings: runtime records the verdict and resumes the root model with concrete findings; the root then repairs/reverifies before another terminal attempt.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: detect recurring operational demand across distinct successful sessions and construct a candidate reusable Skill for future work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated successful multi-step workflows remain ad hoc and repeatedly consume reasoning/tool orchestration instead of becoming a reusable future capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: recognize that a normalized successful tool sequence has repeated across enough distinct sessions and emit a proposed reusable Skill representation with validation evidence. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party ReuseDraftCollector owns detection/candidate generation; no autonomous or parent actor is wired to admit the candidate into the live skill catalog at this ref. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: successful-root-trace extraction, canonical tool/value-shape normalization, stable signature, distinct-session threshold, review artifact generation, persisted SKILL.md candidate and validation examples; separate SkillStore discovery/live reload for actual admitted skill roots. - Closure path: successful root sessions → repeated normalized trace crosses threshold → Bamboo emits review-only reuse-drafts/&lt;candidate&gt;/SKILL.md and validation artifact → constructor boundary stops here. The draft is intentionally outside every discovery root, so an additional promotion decision/path is required before later runs can use it as a live Skill. - Boundary reachability: repeated-trace observation is wired directly into first-party SkillManager and produces the candidate without downstream code. Because admission into current capability is deliberately omitted, the shipped path establishes S4 construction but not autonomous or parent-governed closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: candidate detection/generation is deterministic rather than an autonomous S4 actor, and neither the root model nor a first-party parent approval surface is shown owning promotion into live capability. This is therefore C, not A/P. - Evidence: crates/infra/bamboo-skills/src/reusedraft.rs; crates/infra/bamboo-skills/src/lib.rs; crates/infra/bamboo-skills/src/store/mod.rs; builtinskills/skill-creator/SKILL.md; crates/engine/bamboo-engine/src/gardener.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: user-directed skill-creator can produce and iterate Skill artifacts, and SkillStore live-reloads actual discovery roots, but no first-party reviewed path was found that promotes automatic reuse drafts into those roots. Memory gardeners maintain memory quality rather than providing this missing adaptation authority. - External distinction: repeated successful root sessions encode recurring user/environment demand across distinct operating episodes rather than one current task; the reuse collector deliberately aggregates across distinct sessions. - Future / prospective distinction: the generated artifact is explicitly a proposed reusable Skill intended to change how future matching work can be performed. - Adaptation option generated: a proposed Skill definition/SKILL.md plus validation artifact derived from the recurring tool sequence. - Path back into current capability / S3: not closed at this ref; drafts are intentionally written beside, not inside, the configured Skill discovery root. Promotion into a live-reloaded Skill root requires an additional composition/authority path.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure was established at the declared Bamboo deployment boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Bamboo exposes many task goals, system instructions, permission policies, tool/skill disablement settings and human approvals, but these regulate current actions/capabilities rather than resolving the identity or ultimate-policy questions of the organization itself. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying identity/ultimate-policy right and closed return path were established. - Decision owner: none established for S5 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system prompts, permission checker/approvals, tool/skill configuration, Gold goal configuration, updategoal, runtime budgets and Guardian/skill constructor policies. - Closure path: no qualifying S5 issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative identity/policy decision → returned operational-governance loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the root model can make task decisions and report goal completion, but it does not own the system&#39;s identity or ultimate policy. Operator configuration/approval likewise constrains actions without evidencing S5 closure. - Evidence: crates/engine/bamboo-tools/src/tools/goal.rs; crates/engine/bamboo-engine/src/runtime/config.rs; README.md; inspected server permission/configuration and skill/memory surfaces. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/311ccc4918a19b810674ae2685de8f0bded93a88/assessments/bamboo.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 311ccc4918a19b810674ae2685de8f0bded93a88\">311ccc4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T19:26:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/andrew-yangy/gru-ai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">gruAI</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/andrew-yangy/gru-ai/tree/8fba47928a0fe3fdfda7a31d22b73625c78cbfc4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8fba47928a0fe3fdfda7a31d22b73625c78cbfc4\">8fba479 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: model-driven specialist engineering, auditing, designing and other directive tasks within their scoped project contexts, with tool observations and revision cycles. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing requirements, source defects, failing tests, review findings and variable task complexity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose next code/document/tool action on the scoped goal and revise it after tool/reviewer observations. - Decision owner: spawned model-based Claude Code role agents (engineer/QA/CTO etc.) operating under first-party .claude/agents personas and /directive work instructions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Claude Code CLI tool executor, role templates, task Definition of Done, isolated worktrees, step status JSON, reviews and safety hooks. - Closure path: assigned goal/DOD → spawned model worker selects actions and reads results → evaluator returns issues → worker fixes/rechecks → completed artifact or escalation to orchestrator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task-specific decisions reside in model roles under the shipped task contract; the CLI adapter only launches them and does not autonomously solve tasks. - Evidence: README pipeline, /directive, 09-execute-projects.md, scripts/spawn-agent.ts, role personas and Claude CLI adapter. - Basis: explicit source-to-spawn-to-review control path, not an inferred model feature from names. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: live provider quality and all claimed automated end-to-end runs unverified; role sessions require installed Claude Code and access. - Boundary reachability: the init scaffold installs the skill/roles and /directive invokes them through the supported CLI adapter; results feed project state and review phases.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent concrete cross-worker file/edit collisions while allowing parallel non-overlapping S1 work, and enforce separation of builder/reviewer roles. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two builders changing overlapping files, global configuration file edits and incompatible simultaneous dependency work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: sequence conflicting S1 tasks into different waves and allow only disjoint file scopes to execute in one parallel wave. - Decision owner: constructor-specified computeWaves algorithm in the shipped execution protocol, executed by the orchestrating agent; static global sequential file set and file-overlap rule bound decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: audit activefiles[], task dependencies, MAXPARALLEL=3, overlap detection, post-wave changed-file comparison and reviewer identity validation. - Closure path: pending S1 task scopes → compute dependency/file-conflict wave → launch safe workers → compare changed files, review/repair → subsequent wave eligible after prior tasks finish. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the attenuation rule is constructor-authored, not a free model-authorized cross-team strategy; nonetheless it addresses specific interference between autonomous S1 writers. - Distinct S1 units: per-task Claude Code builder sessions, including concurrently active specialist agents on the same project tree. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping activefiles or shared global config operations can corrupt/overwrite another worker&#39;s changes. - Attenuating coordination relation: deterministic wave planner refuses concurrent file intersections and serializes global modification files, bounded by dependency readiness. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: waves complete and changed files are compared to the predicted set; remaining tasks enter later waves only after prior completions, and reviews feed repairs back to builders. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the explicit file-overlap interference model changes which otherwise-ready independent S1 tasks can act simultaneously, preventing destructive concurrent edits rather than merely passing messages. - Evidence: 09-execute-projects.md wave algorithm, 05-planning.md cast validation, .claude/hooks/validate-cast.sh and validate-reviews.sh. - Basis: explicit normative executable instructions plus gate scripts in the installed workflow. - Confidence: medium; protocol specifies the algorithm but there is no independent measured production collision benchmark at this pinned source. - Caveats: post-wave drift is warning-only; S2=C is bounded to specified scheduling and role constraints, not proof of comprehensive runtime concurrency safety. - Boundary reachability: /directive execute phase requires the wave manifest and uses scripts/spawn-agent.ts for parallel CLI builders; conflicts influence later planned waves.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: model-led COO orchestration of the present directive portfolio: challenge scope, audit state, assign role agents, select plan, reprioritize, review outcomes and escalate when gates fail. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing goals, active file scope, task dependencies, unavailable workers, repeated review failures, cost and inconsistent project progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select and revise the current execution plan/cast across multiple specialist S1 workers, determine when to stop/rescope and return a corrected directive state. - Decision owner: the first-party COO model persona and directive orchestrator under the user&#39;s approved intent, with CTO/QA evidence inputs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: audit artifacts, plan.json, project.json, directive.json, step dependency guards, reviewer artifacts, task statuses, dashboard watcher and scripted scheduler capacity cap. - Closure path: CEO directive/current state → audit/COO whole-plan judgment → assign and sequence specialist tasks → collect review and completion feedback → adjust/escalate or present digest to CEO. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the COO decides the active plan, casting, phasing and tradeoffs from source/audit evidence; separate scripts only validate/record that current control. - Whole-system current view: directive goals, projects, DOD statuses, candidate agents, current backlog, lessons and budget/role constraints assembled before COO plan and subsequent reports. - Current-control decision scope: model-based allocation of builders/reviewers, sequencing, risk/escalation and present directive work; external CEO alone owns final acceptance and high-risk authorization. - Evidence: morgan-coo.md, /directive/SKILL.md, 05-planning.md, 09-execute-projects.md, 10-wrapup.md and README. - Basis: encoded role/planning and return architecture with checkable state contracts. - Confidence: medium-high for available mode; no production performance assertion. - Caveats: this is bounded current work control, not business-wide governance of every linked product or independent authority to approve a release. - Boundary reachability: /directive explicitly invokes COO planning, writes approved project plan, launches roles, reads returned reviews and progresses to a digest/CEO gate.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: mandatory task peer review, QA and mechanical casting/review validations improve quality but do not evidence an independent complementary audit institution of the whole S3 control loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: self-review, incomplete DOD, hidden implementation regressions and omitted verification. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently test the current S3 controller&#39;s account of actual organization-wide operations and return a binding management challenge. - Decision owner: assigned CTO/reviewer inside normal task workflow; no separate first-party independent S3 actor with complementary access to S3 is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh-context code-review prompts, validate-reviews.sh, validate-cast.sh, project DOD and review artifacts. - Closure path: builder artifact → reviewer observations → builder repair/ordinary gate → COO completion flow; not a distinct system-wide S3 → S3 challenge path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: independent task review is not automatically independent organizational audit; reviewers are selected by the current planning chain. - Evidence: 09-execute-projects.md, validate-reviews.sh, 05-planning.md. - Basis: separate task-check vs complementary whole-system audit test. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: product claims about fresh contexts do not independently attest reviewer independence in a deployed environment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: model-led prospective scanning of external technology, competition, user and ecosystem signals, with synthesis into prioritized future directives and possible operational adaptation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changes to frameworks/security advisories, competitor moves, emerging markets, user needs and prospective workflow technologies. - Decisive decision or feedback right: autonomously select external research questions, weigh competing signals and propose initiatives/capability changes for authorized backlog/directive promotion. - Decision owner: first-party CTO/CPO/CMO/COO model scouts and COO consolidator; human CEO retains acceptance of ordinary proposals and high-risk decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WebSearch/WebFetch instructions, prior scouting archive, goal alignment, urgency schema, cross-scout signal strengths, backlog triggers, /scout and scheduled claude -p entry. - Closure path: external developments → multi-domain agent research → independent proposed actions → COO cross-domain relevance/urgency synthesis → CEO review or bounded strong-signal inbox promotion → approved directive executed by S1/S3 pipeline. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: research queries, interpretation, consolidation and prioritized option selection are explicit model-agent responsibilities; script scheduling and CEO gate are support and authorization, respectively. - Prospective environment view: technology CVEs and releases, competition/customer signals, growth landscape and workflow ecosystem not just current repo health. - Future option/adaptation decision: propose ranked specific initiatives with scope, evidence, risk and process; cross-reference pending backlog triggers to promote actionable work. - Return into operating capability: proposals accepted by CEO, plus specified strong signals, create/promo inbox directives that the first-party /directive workflow can act on; adoption is not guaranteed without authorization. - External distinction: scouts search competitors, platform advisories, market/customer signals and future framework choices, rather than merely the local build&#39;s current defects. - Future / prospective distinction: they assess changes that might affect later product viability and workflow capabilities; the goal is option selection before commitment, not incident response to an already assigned task. - Adaptation option generated: source schema requires evidence-linked prioritized initiatives with risk, recommended process and scope; COO can promote validated multi-scout signals to inbox directives. - Path back into current capability / S3: approved or bounded automatically promoted initiative becomes a directive that the active COO-and-specialists operating workflow can plan and execute, subject to CEO gates. - Evidence: /scout/SKILL.md, scripts/scheduled-runs.sh, COO persona, /directive consumption path and README. - Basis: implemented, reachable prospective scout protocol with bounded return, not mere roadmap language. - Confidence: medium; live search/approval/execution was not performed. - Caveats: hardcoded example installation paths require deployment configuration; not proof autonomous approval of strategic changes, and model-generated prospective findings may be wrong. - Boundary reachability: /scout is shipped as a first-party skill, scripts/scheduled-runs.sh scout invokes it via Claude CLI, and its initiatives/trigger promotions feed the same .context directive/backlog structure used by normal execution.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: the CEO&#39;s vision, standing preferences, approval gates and agent personas constrain execution but do not create first-party model authority to resolve ultimate identity/policy conflicts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous purpose, unsafe high-risk changes, conflicting priorities and mistakes in approval or scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: legitimately adjudicate contested mission/identity and constitutional policy, then bind the operating organization to the decision. - Decision owner: external human CEO defines vision and must approve plan/completion; model personas obey inherited constraints and cannot unilaterally change them. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: .context/vision.md, preferences, triage/classification rules, human clarification/plan/completion stop gates and validate-gate.sh. - Closure path: external CEO decision → scaffolded policy and approval status → bounded S1/S3 execution; no independent first-party ultimate-policy judgment and constitution-changing return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: naming the user CEO and the model COO does not demonstrate that software legitimately owns organizational S5. The explicit mandatory human gate identifies the outside owner. - Evidence: /directive/SKILL.md, 11-completion-gate.md, 07-plan-approval.md, README. - Basis: decision-right and human-stop trace. - Confidence: high for bounded exclusion. - Caveats: real-world team could include its human CEO in a broader assessed organization; no automatic parent-assisted autonomy credit is inferred here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3da53146a6df1cee901878d25327deb8991d105e/assessments/gr/gruai.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3da53146a6df1cee901878d25327deb8991d105e\">3da5314 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
